<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">The story of Severi–Brauer varieties ties together three—seemingly unrelated—strands.</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Central simple algebras</span> are finite dimensional, simple algebras, whose center is a given field <math id="p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The first example is the quaternions, discovered by Hamilton in 1843. Wedderburn’s
contributions started in 1905, and the subject grew into its own around
1925 with the works of Albert, Brauer, Hasse and Noether.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">vdW91</a>, Chap.13]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jac80</a>, Chap.4]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan02</a>, Chap.XVII]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>]</cite>
or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta22</a>, Chap.073W]</cite>
for introductions.</p>
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Severi–Brauer varieties</span> are varieties defined over a field <math id="p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, that become isomorphic to projective space over the algebraic closure <math id="p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Plane conics without <math id="p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points give the ‘baby examples,’ though they were usually viewed as part of the theory of quadratic forms.
Higher dimensional Severi–Brauer varieties
were first defined and studied by Severi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sev1932</a>]</cite>, and their theory came into full maturity starting with Châtelet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Châ44</a>]</cite>, who used both Galois cohomology and central simple algebras.
For a variety <math id="p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
maps to non-trivial Severi–Brauer varieties give the best understood obstruction to having a <math id="p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point. Thus Severi–Brauer varieties appear especially frequently in arithmetic questions, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">CTS21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(k,{\mathbb{G}}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(k,{\mathbb{G}}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, the second Galois cohomology group of the multiplicative group <math id="p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the most powerful viewpoint
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p6.1" class="ltx_p">The aim of these notes is to present a geometric treatment of Severi–Brauer varieties, without using any results from the theory of central simple algebras or Galois cohomology. This reverses the
treatments prevalent since <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Châ44</a>]</cite>, which start either with
central simple algebras or with Galois cohomology, and proceed to derive geometric properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p7.1" class="ltx_p">A series of <span id="p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asides</span> outline either
connections to the other approaches to Severi–Brauer varieties, or discuss connections with other topics.
These are not needed for the proofs, but provide motivation and hints for those who intend to go deeper into some of these topics.</p>
</div>
<div id="p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p8.1" class="ltx_p">Introductions to the algebraic and cohomological methods are in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mil80</a>, Chap.IV]</cite>.
The arithmetic aspects are treated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">CTS21</a>]</cite>.
Connections with algebraic geometry are discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro68</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AHVAV17</a>]</cite>, while
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">KO81</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver82</a>]</cite> are more algebraic.</p>
</div>
<div id="p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p9.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea of our treatment is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assertion">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assertion 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The geometry of a Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is best studied via the vector bundle
<math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> which is obtained as the unique (up-to isomorphism) non-split extension</p>
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S0.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm1" title="Assertion 1. ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the tangent bundle of <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p10.1" class="ltx_p">The vector bundle <math id="p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> appears in Quillen’s work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qui73</a>, §8.4]</cite>
(with a different construction), a similar bundle is used in
an unpublished preprint of Szabó
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sza06</a>]</cite> (which also contains the first steps of a program to treat
Severi–Brauer varieties geometrically), and is a special case of the
absolutely split bundles studied by
Biswas–Nagaraj <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN07</a>]</cite> and
Novaković <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nov24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Description of the Sections</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p12.1" class="ltx_p">We start in Section <a href="#S1" title="1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by studying the key abstract property of the vector bundle <math id="p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math>: it is a direct sum of copies of <math id="p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p13.1" class="ltx_p">The first examples of Severi–Brauer varieties are exhibited in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Basic properties of twisted linear subvareties are established in
Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and splitting fields are constructed in
Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p14.1" class="ltx_p">We show in Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> that the examples of Section <a href="#S2" title="2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
give all non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties, and that
<math id="p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Aut}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Aut}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on flags of twisted linear subvareties.</p>
</div>
<div id="p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p15.1" class="ltx_p">Twisted linear systems are studied in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, leading to a geometric definition of the Brauer group in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
The index and the period of Severi–Brauer varieties is studied in Section <a href="#S8" title="8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p16.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S9" title="9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> we construct
cyclic Severi–Brauer varieties from hypersurfaces
of degree <math id="p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and norm forms.</p>
</div>
<div id="p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p17.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S10" title="10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> we construct
the sequence (<a href="#Thmthm1" title="Assertion 1. ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.1) for Severi–Brauer schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmackx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ack">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmackx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments</span></span><span id="Thmackx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmackx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmackx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">I thank J.-L. Colliot-Thélène, A.J. de Jong, P. Gille, D. Litt, S.  Novaković, M. Shin, C. Skinner, J. Starr and
T. Szamuely for many
comments, corrections and references.
Partial financial support was provided by the NSF (grant number
DMS-1901855) and by the Simons Foundation (grant number SFI-MPS-MOV-00006719-02).</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Geometrically split vector bundles</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we study abstract properties of the vector bundle <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#Thmthm1" title="Assertion 1. ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.1). Its key feature is that, while
<math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is usually indecomposable over the ground field <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, it is a direct sum of several copies of a line bundle over <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our main interest is in smooth projective varieties, but
the basic results in this section hold in the following setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> a fixed algebraic closure of <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme that is geometrically reduced and geometrically connected. (That is, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced and connected.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The basic results all work if <math id="Thmthm2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is not proper, as long as <math id="Thmthm2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(X,{\mathcal{O}}_{X})\cong k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(X,{\mathcal{O}}_{X})\cong k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given
a line bundle <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the category of vector bundles <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that
<math id="Thmthm2.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{\bar{k}}\cong\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bar{k}}\cong\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>; a sum of copies of <math id="Thmthm2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmthm2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="Thmthm2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted line bundle</span> on <math id="Thmthm2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="Thmthm2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a
nonzero vector bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be twisted line bundles on <math id="Thmthm2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\neq F_{i}\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq F_{i}\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}\otimes F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{1}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}\otimes F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{1}\otimes{\mathcal{L}}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus all twisted line bundles on <math id="Thmthm2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> form a group
<math id="Thmthm2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="Thmthm2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside.</span> We note in (<a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.4) that <math id="Thmthm2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)=\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏𝐢𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)=\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="Thmthm2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points of the Picard scheme of <math id="Thmthm2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A line bundle <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with the
twisted line bundle <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}:=L_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}:=L_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We see in (<a href="#Thmthm8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) that in this case
<math id="Thmthm2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> consists of sums of copies of <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, if <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a rank 1 bundle <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmthm2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\cong L_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\cong L_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus there is a natural injection</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}(X)\lhook\joinrel\to\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}(X)\lhook\joinrel\to\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmthm2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}}\otimes M_{\bar{k}})=T({\mathcal{L}})\otimes M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}}\otimes M_{\bar{k}})=T({\mathcal{L}})\otimes M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus twisted line bundles really study the quotient
<math id="Thmthm2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)/\operatorname{Pic}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)/\operatorname{Pic}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Technically it is better to work with a separable algebraic closure <math id="Thmthm2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of an algebraic closure <math id="Thmthm2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that <math id="Thmthm2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}=k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}=k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmthm2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{char}k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>char</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{char}k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, or if
<math id="Thmthm2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{char}k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>char</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{char}k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect.)
We prove in (<a href="#Thmthm35" title="Lemma 35. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>)
that the two versions are equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our definition of twisted line bundles is related to, but quite different from,
the notion of twisted coherent sheaves studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yos06</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lie07</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The simplest example of a twisted line bundle is <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> of a plane conic.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_babyexmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baby Example 3</span></span><span id="Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety such that <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{1}_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(-K_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(-K_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample and gives an embedding
<math id="Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\lhook\joinrel\to{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⸦→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\lhook\joinrel\to{\mathbb{P}}^{2}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> whose image is a conic; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KSC04</a>, Secs.1.2–3]</cite> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="Thmthm3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="Thmthm3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points.
(For example, if <math id="Thmthm3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\subset{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\subset{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> then we can take
<math id="Thmthm3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has no degree 1 line bundles defined over <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (since the zero set of any section would be a <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point), but we have
<math id="Thmthm3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2)={\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{2}}(1)|_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2)={\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{2}}(1)|_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore,
<math id="Thmthm3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}\bigl(C,{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(-2)\bigr)\cong k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}\bigl(C,{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(-2)\bigr)\cong k</annotation></semantics></math>, thus we have a non-split extension</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm3" title="Baby Example 3. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\to F(C)\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2)\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\to F(C)\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(2)\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We know that, over <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to
<math id="Thmthm3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)+{\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)+{\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(C)</annotation></semantics></math> itself is an indecomposable vector bundle over <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(C)\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(C)\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="Thmthm3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}:={\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}:={\mathcal{O}}_{C_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We see next that it is better to think of (<a href="#Thmthm3" title="Baby Example 3. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.1) as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm3" title="Baby Example 3. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\to F(C)\to T_{C}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{C}\to F(C)\to T_{C}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Severi–Brauer variety)<span id="Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Severi–Brauer variety</span> over a field <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a
variety <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraic closure of <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We see in (<a href="#Thmthm35" title="Lemma 35. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) that this is equivalent
to <math id="Thmthm4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is a separable algebraic closure of <math id="Thmthm4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is not easy to write down explicit Severi–Brauer varieties of dimension <math id="Thmthm4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> or even to show that they exist; see Sections <a href="#S2" title="2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S9" title="9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> for some examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More generally,
let <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field extension and <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme.
A <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-form of <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cong Y\times_{\operatorname{Spec}k}\operatorname{Spec}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cong Y\times_{\operatorname{Spec}k}\operatorname{Spec}K</annotation></semantics></math>; we abbreviate this as <math id="Thmthm4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cong Y_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cong Y_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ser59</a>, Sec.V.20]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ser88</a>, Sec.V.20]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">GW10</a>, pp.456-463]</cite>
for general discussions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Severi–Brauer varieties over <math id="Thmthm4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="Thmthm4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-forms of <math id="Thmthm4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_mainexmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Example 5</span></span><span id="Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmthm5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> of a Severi–Brauer variety)<span id="Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety over a field <math id="Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As in the 1-dimensional case, <math id="Thmthm5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> is very ample and gives an embedding
<math id="Thmthm5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\lhook\joinrel\to{\mathbb{P}}^{N}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⸦→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\lhook\joinrel\to{\mathbb{P}}^{N}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\binom{2n+1}{n}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\binom{2n+1}{n}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, but this is very hard to use since the codimension of <math id="Thmthm5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is large.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The tangent bundle of <math id="Thmthm5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be presented using the Euler sequence</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\to 0;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mover><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>e</mi></mover><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\to 0;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">EH16</a>, 3.2.4]</cite> or
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har77</a>, II.8.13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the sequence (<a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.1) is determined by
its nonzero extension class</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})=H^{1}({{\mathbb{P}}^{n}},\Omega_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}^{1})\cong k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})=H^{1}({{\mathbb{P}}^{n}},\Omega_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}^{1})\cong k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, for any Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{P},{\mathcal{O}}_{P})=H^{1}(P,\Omega_{P}^{1})\cong k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{P},{\mathcal{O}}_{P})=H^{1}(P,\Omega_{P}^{1})\cong k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p4.3" class="ltx_p">(It is not important for now, but in (<a href="#Thmthm83" title="Corollary 83. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">83</span></a>) we write down a
canonical isomorphism <math id="Thmthm5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(P,\Omega_{P}^{1})\cong k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(P,\Omega_{P}^{1})\cong k</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We can thus define a vector bundle <math id="Thmthm5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> as the unique (up-to scaling) non-split extension</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmthm5.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(P)_{\bar{k}}\cong\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)_{\bar{k}}\cong\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, thus
<math id="Thmthm5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The Picard group of
<math id="Thmthm5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}[{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)]\cong{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}[{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)]\cong{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but, as in the baby example,
there is usually no line bundle <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, we see during the first proof of (<a href="#Thmthm14" title="Corollary 14 (Châtelet’s theorem). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) that <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> exists as a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> iff <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(In most cases, the only line bundles on <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are given by (multiples of)
the canonical class <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalently, by (powers of) the dualizing sheaf <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(K_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(K_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>
correspond to <math id="Thmthm5.p6.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-\dim P-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-\dim P-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This gives us
the main examples of varieties with twisted line bundles: <math id="Thmthm5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=P</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(P):=T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)\quad\mbox{and let}\quad E(P):=E\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and let</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(P):=T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)\quad\mbox{and let}\quad E(P):=E\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">denote a minimal rank direct summand of <math id="Thmthm5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(We see in (<a href="#Thmthm8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) that <math id="Thmthm5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to isomorphism.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">It follows from
(<a href="#Thmthm12" title="Corollary 12. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) and (<a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>.2) that the examples <math id="Thmthm5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> are universal: every other
twisted line bundle is obtained from some <math id="Thmthm5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> by pull-back and tensoring by a (non-twisted) line bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note.</span> We chose to write everyting in terms of the
twisted line bundle <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> since the resulting vector bundle <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> fits in the exact sequence (<a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.4). One could also use its dual
<math id="Thmthm5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. That would give <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> which is related to the cotangent bundle <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, the other twisted line bundles <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> also lead to interesting vector bundles. If <math id="Thmthm5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\dim P+1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\dim P+1)=1</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="Thmthm5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(m)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(m)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially equivalent to
<math id="Thmthm5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_vardefs">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant Definitions 6</span></span><span id="Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although we will only deal with <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> as defined above,
the definition makes sense if <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal and <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
rank 1 reflexive sheaf. All the properties in this note hold in that setting. This is sometimes convenient in studying the pull-back of a line bundle by a rational map <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\dasharrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\dasharrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth then <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally extends to a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but if
<math id="Thmthm6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is only normal then <math id="Thmthm6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> usually extends only to a
rank 1 reflexive sheaf.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The definition and the basic properties also work when
<math id="Thmthm6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by a <span id="Thmthm6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">simple</span> coherent sheaf <math id="Thmthm6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Simple means that <math id="Thmthm6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}({\mathcal{G}})\cong\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}({\mathcal{G}})\cong\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is also of interest to investigate bundles <math id="Thmthm6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of line bundles of different degrees.
The study of such bundles on Severi–Brauer varieties was started in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN07</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BN09</a>]</cite> and completed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nov24</a>]</cite>, where they are called <span id="Thmthm6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">absolutely split</span> bundles. These bundles play a key role in understanding the bounded derived category of coherent sheaves on Severi–Brauer varieties; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BSS11</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nov17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Variant Definitions 6. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.1.</span> One can also consider those bundles <math id="Thmthm6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <span id="Thmthm6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extension</span> of copies of <math id="Thmthm6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, or even of line bundles numerically equivalent to <math id="Thmthm6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a better notion on Abelian varieties, though the resulting category is not semisimple;
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muk81</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH09</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bri13</a>]</cite> for various characterizations and uses of these vector bundles.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 7</span></span><span id="Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be any <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> a coherent sheaf on <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> a Galois extension.
Then <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is a coherent sheaf on <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{K}^{\sigma}\cong F_{K}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>σ</mi></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for every</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{K}^{\sigma}\cong F_{K}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Conversely, let <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a coherent sheaf on <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We would like to know whether <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}\cong F_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}\cong F_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>
for some coherent sheaf <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. A clear necessary condition is</p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{F}}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for every</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{F}}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These conditions are usually not sufficient, and Grothendieck’s descent theory
provides the full answer; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR90</a>, Chap.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another approach is to try to understand all coherent sheaves that
satisfy the naive descent conditions (<a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.2).
We claim that for line bundles, this corresponds exactly to the twisted ones.
(We prove in (<a href="#Thmthm35" title="Lemma 35. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>)
that we can go from <math id="Thmthm7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}/k</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmthm7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}/k</annotation></semantics></math> in the definition of
twisted line bundles.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Claim <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.3.</span> Let <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper, geometrically reduced and geometrically connected <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme and <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted line bundle on <math id="Thmthm7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> iff</p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for every</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}\quad\mbox{for every}\quad\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Pick <math id="Thmthm7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\neq F\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq F\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every
<math id="Thmthm7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}\cong F_{k^{s}}\cong F_{k^{s}}^{\sigma}\cong\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}\cong F_{k^{s}}\cong F_{k^{s}}^{\sigma}\cong\oplus_{i}{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus there is a nonzero map <math id="Thmthm7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying powers of <math id="Thmthm7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> until we reach <math id="Thmthm7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{m}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get a sequence of nonzero maps</p>
<table id="S1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma^{2}}\to\cdots\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma^{m}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma^{2}}\to\cdots\to{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma^{m}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The composite is an isomorphism, hence all intermediate maps are isomorphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, let <math id="Thmthm7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite Galois extension.
Then <math id="Thmthm7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\otimes_{k}K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\otimes_{k}K</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of
<math id="Thmthm7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)|</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="Thmthm7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>; projections to the summands correspond to
the bilinear maps <math id="Thmthm7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\mapsto a^{\sigma}\cdot b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\mapsto a^{\sigma}\cdot b</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmthm7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZS58</a>, Secs.III.14-15]</cite>
for a thorough discussion of tensor products of fields.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> as above, let <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:X_{K}\to X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:X_{K}\to X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural morphism and <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> a coherent sheaf on <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}p_{*}F=(p_{*}F)\otimes_{k}K\cong F\otimes_{K}K\otimes_{k}K\cong\oplus_{\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)}F^{\sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>K</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>K</mi></msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}p_{*}F=(p_{*}F)\otimes_{k}K\cong F\otimes_{K}K\otimes_{k}K\cong\oplus_{\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)}F^{\sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm7.p6.2" class="ltx_p">This shows that if <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for every <math id="Thmthm7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="Thmthm7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}{\mathcal{L}}\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}{\mathcal{L}}\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We can reformulate the above result in terms of the Picard <span id="Thmthm7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scheme</span> <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏𝐢𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. (See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mum66</a>, Lect.19]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLR90</a>, Chap.8]</cite> for introductions to the Picard scheme. We will not use any of it in the sequel.)
We get that the class of <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏𝐢𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is
invariant under <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, <math id="Thmthm7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathcal{L}}]\in\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏𝐢𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathcal{L}}]\in\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This shows that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S1.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)=\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏𝐢𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)=\operatorname{\mathbf{Pic}}(X)(k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next we prove some quite elementary but surprisingly useful results about the category <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8</span></span><span id="Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notation and assumptions are as in
Definition <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Then all morphisms in <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> split and there is a unique vector bundle
<math id="Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that every other member of <math id="Thmthm8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of copies of
<math id="Thmthm8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Let us start over <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}({\mathcal{L}},{\mathcal{L}})\cong\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}({\mathcal{L}},{\mathcal{L}})\cong\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, morphisms <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi:\oplus_{i=1}^{n}{\mathcal{L}}\to\oplus_{j=1}^{m}{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\oplus_{i=1}^{n}{\mathcal{L}}\to\oplus_{j=1}^{m}{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent to an <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
with entries in <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus kernels and cokernels are also
sums of copies of <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> and all morphisms split.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let now <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1},F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:F_{1}\to F_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:F_{1}\to F_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> a morphism.
Then <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a coherent sheaf such that
<math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ker\phi)_{\bar{k}}=\ker\bigl(\phi_{\bar{k}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ker\phi)_{\bar{k}}=\ker\bigl(\phi_{\bar{k}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of copies of <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is locally free and it is in <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Same for
<math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{coker}\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{coker}\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">An exact sequence of sheaves <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to F_{1}\to F_{2}\to F_{3}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to F_{1}\to F_{2}\to F_{3}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> is classified by an extension class
<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(F_{3},F_{1})=H^{1}(X,F_{1}\otimes F_{3}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(F_{3},F_{1})=H^{1}(X,F_{1}\otimes F_{3}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We already know that <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, hence so is <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.
(Note that we do not claim that if <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1},F_{3}\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},F_{3}\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>, this is false if <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X,{\mathcal{O}}_{X})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X,{\mathcal{O}}_{X})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally let <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector bundle in <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> with smallest positive rank
and <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> any vector bundle in <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a
nonzero map <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:E({\mathcal{L}})\to F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:E({\mathcal{L}})\to F</annotation></semantics></math> since there is one over <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker\phi\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker\phi\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is an injection and
<math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong E({\mathcal{L}})+F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong E({\mathcal{L}})+F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. By induction on the rank we get that
<math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of copies of
<math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 9</span></span><span id="Thmthm9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="Thmthm9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl(\det E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)_{\bar{k}}\cong\det\bigl(\oplus_{1}^{e}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{e}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\det E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)_{\bar{k}}\cong\det\bigl(\oplus_{1}^{e}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{e}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> should be thought of as the
<math id="Thmthm9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>th tensor power of <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is defined on <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the twisted line bundle <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>
has 2 ‘shadows’ on <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})\quad\mbox{and}\quad{\mathcal{L}}^{(e)}:=\det E({\mathcal{L}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})\quad\mbox{and}\quad{\mathcal{L}}^{(e)}:=\det E({\mathcal{L}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm9.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Using these, we will be able to work as if <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>
were a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 10</span></span><span id="Thmthm10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmthm10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmthm10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore,</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10.1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a skew field,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10.2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(\oplus_{i=1}^{r}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(\oplus_{i=1}^{r}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> matrix
algebra over </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10.3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> iff </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a skew field,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(10.4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">there is a natural correspondence between left ideals </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset\operatorname{End}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset\operatorname{End}(F)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
direct summands </span><math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, given by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I\mapsto G(I):=\cap_{A\in I}\ker A,\quad\mbox{and}\quad G\mapsto I(G):=\{A:G\subset\ker A\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ker</mi><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ker</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\mapsto G(I):=\cap_{A\in I}\ker A,\quad\mbox{and}\quad G\mapsto I(G):=\{A:G\subset\ker A\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. In order to compute dimensions, note that
<math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}(F)=H^{0}\bigl(X,{\mathchanc{End}}(F)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}(F)=H^{0}\bigl(X,{\mathchanc{End}}(F)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathchanc{End}}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathchanc{End}}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sheaf of endomorphisms, thus</p>
<table id="S1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=h^{0}\bigl(X_{\bar{k}},{\mathchanc{End}}(F)_{\bar{k}}\bigr)=h^{0}\bigl(X_{\bar{k}},{\mathchanc{End}}(F_{\bar{k}})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=h^{0}\bigl(X_{\bar{k}},{\mathchanc{End}}(F)_{\bar{k}}\bigr)=h^{0}\bigl(X_{\bar{k}},{\mathchanc{End}}(F_{\bar{k}})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\operatorname{rank}F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\operatorname{rank}F)</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathchanc{End}}(F_{\bar{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathchanc{End}}(F_{\bar{k}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{X_{\bar{k}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{X_{\bar{k}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{k}\operatorname{End}(F)=(\operatorname{rank}F)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As we noted above, every endomorphism <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:E({\mathcal{L}})\to E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:E({\mathcal{L}})\to E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is either zero or an isomorphism, thus <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew field, and <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(\oplus_{i=1}^{r}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(\oplus_{i=1}^{r}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
algebra over <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.3) says that
<math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> iff
there are zero-divisors in the <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times r</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Finally the natural correspondence between its left ideals and
subspaces of <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is
(<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.4). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">This is the first appearence of skew fields in our approach to
Severi–Brauer varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 11</span></span><span id="Thmthm11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the theory of central simple algebras,
<span id="Thmthm11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Brauer equivalence</span> is obtained by declaring an algebra <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
to be equivalent to its matrix algebras <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{r}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{r}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) says that
the algebras <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> are Brauer equivalent to each other for <math id="Thmthm11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in T({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in T({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 12</span></span><span id="Thmthm12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be another proper,
geometrically reduced and connected <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:Y\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:Y\to X</annotation></semantics></math> a morphism. Then <math id="Thmthm12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\bigl(p^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)\cong p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\bigl(p^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)\cong p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. It is clear that <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\in T\bigl(p^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\in T\bigl(p^{*}{\mathcal{L}}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, the only question is whether <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> has minimal rank or not. We have a natural algebra morphism
<math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)\to\operatorname{End}\bigl(p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)\to\operatorname{End}\bigl(p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) the two have the same dimension, and
<math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew field. Thus
<math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)\to\operatorname{End}\bigl(p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)\to\operatorname{End}\bigl(p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism, and
so <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> is also indecomposable by (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.3). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 13</span></span><span id="Thmthm13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmthm13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(k)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(k)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> then the
natural injection
<math id="Thmthm13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}(X)\lhook\joinrel\to\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Pic</mi><mi>tw</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}(X)\lhook\joinrel\to\operatorname{Pic}^{\rm tw}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> given in (<a href="#Thmthm2" title="Definition 2. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.1) is an isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Proof.
Pick any twisted line bundle <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
apply (<a href="#Thmthm12" title="Corollary 12. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) to <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\operatorname{Spec}k\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\operatorname{Spec}k\to X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Over <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}k</annotation></semantics></math> an indecomposable vector bundle has rank 1. Thus
<math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and so <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})\in\operatorname{Pic}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})\in\operatorname{Pic}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> show that
<math id="S1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\in\operatorname{Pic}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\in\operatorname{Pic}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 14</span></span><span id="Thmthm14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Châtelet’s theorem)<span id="Thmthm14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a
Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> iff <math id="Thmthm14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">First proof. Assume that <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply (<a href="#Thmthm13" title="Corollary 13. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) to <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We obtain that <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle that becomes
isomorphic to <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its global sections determine a map <math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:P\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:P\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism over <math id="S1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence an isomorphism.
The converse is clear.∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Second proof. Pick local coordinates <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at a <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point of <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the restriction map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcl}r_{p}:H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})\bigr)&amp;\to&amp;H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P}/(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}\bigr)\\
&amp;\cong&amp;{\mathcal{O}}_{P}/(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>≅</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcl}r_{p}:H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})\bigr)&amp;\to&amp;H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P}/(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}\bigr)\\
&amp;\cong&amp;{\mathcal{O}}_{P}/(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p15.2" class="ltx_p">We know that <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(-K_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(-K_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism over <math id="S1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence an isomorphism.
Thus <math id="S1.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique section <math id="S1.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}(s)\equiv x_{1}x_{2}^{n}\mod(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>mod</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}(s)\equiv x_{1}x_{2}^{n}\mod(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p15.3" class="ltx_p">Over <math id="S1.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> we can write <math id="S1.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}+(\mbox{higher terms})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>higher terms</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=\bar{x}_{i}+(\mbox{higher terms})</annotation></semantics></math>
where the <math id="S1.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are linear coordinates. Since</p>
<table id="S1.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{x}_{1}\bar{x}_{2}^{n}\equiv x_{1}x_{2}^{n}\mod(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>mod</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{x}_{1}\bar{x}_{2}^{n}\equiv x_{1}x_{2}^{n}\mod(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})^{n+2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p15.4" class="ltx_p">we conclude that <math id="S1.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s=0)_{\bar{k}}=\bigl(\bar{x}_{1}\bar{x}_{2}^{n}=0\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s=0)_{\bar{k}}=\bigl(\bar{x}_{1}\bar{x}_{2}^{n}=0\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> denote the closure of the smooth locus of <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s=0)</annotation></semantics></math>; it is defined over <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the above computation
<math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the hyperplane <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\bar{x}_{1}=0\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\bar{x}_{1}=0\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the linear system <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|H|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|H|</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphically onto <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 15</span></span><span id="Thmthm15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Wedderburn’s theorem)<span id="Thmthm15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Over a finite field <math id="Thmthm15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> every
Severi–Brauer variety is trivial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. There are several ways to do this but all have some subtlety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">First proof. It is not hard to prove directly that the number of <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-points of <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+q+\cdots+q^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+q+\cdots+q^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim P</annotation></semantics></math> and then
construct an isomorphism <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> step-by-step;
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KSC04</a>, 1.23–26]</cite> for an elementary approach.
We could also use
(<a href="#Thmthm14" title="Corollary 14 (Châtelet’s theorem). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) once we have an <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>-point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Second proof.
By a theorem of Wedderburn, every finite skew-field is commutative.
Thus <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(P)</annotation></semantics></math> has rank 1 over <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), so <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.
The traditional proof of Wedderburn’s theorem is explained in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Her64</a>, Sec.7.2]</cite>, a more powerful argument is in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ser79</a>, Sec.X.7]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>, Sec.6.2]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far the only examples of Severi–Brauer varieties we have are plane conics.
In this section we give 2 methods to construct new
Severi–Brauer varieties out of a given one, and then 2 constructions of Severi–Brauer varieties of dimensions 2 and 3.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16</span></span><span id="Thmthm16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Symmetric powers.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a variety <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> denote its <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th
<span id="Thmthm16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetric power.</span> That is <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)=X^{n}/S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)=X^{n}/S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient of the product of <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> copies on <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by the symmetric group <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> permuting the factors.
We can also think of <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as parametrizing unordered sets of <math id="Thmthm16.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points
<math id="Thmthm16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{n}\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{n}\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, where repetitions are allowed.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm16.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One can identify an unordered set of <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points
<math id="Thmthm16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{n}\in{\mathbb{P}}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{n}\in{\mathbb{P}}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the unique (up to a multiplicative constant) polynomial of degree <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> whose roots are <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus
<math id="Thmthm16.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}({\mathbb{P}}^{1})\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}({\mathbb{P}}^{1})\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, if <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a 1-dimensional
Severi–Brauer variety then <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="Thmthm16.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional
Severi–Brauer variety.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm16.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm16.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2m</annotation></semantics></math> is even then
<math id="Thmthm16.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{2m}(C)\cong|-mK_{C}|\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{2m}(C)\cong|-mK_{C}|\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{2m}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd
and <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> also has no <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-points.
Thus
we get our first examples of nontrivial Severi–Brauer varieties of dimension <math id="Thmthm16.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm16.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm16.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm16.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside <a href="#Thmthm16" title="16Symmetric powers. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>.1.</span> Springer’s theorem says that if a quadric
hypersurface has a point in an odd degree field extension of <math id="Thmthm16.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, then it has a
<math id="Thmthm16.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> point. For 1-dimensional quadrics this is especially easy to prove:
if <math id="Thmthm16.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\subset C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\subset C</annotation></semantics></math> is a point of degree <math id="Thmthm16.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r+1</annotation></semantics></math>, then any section of
<math id="Thmthm16.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(P+rK_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{C}(P+rK_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes at a <math id="Thmthm16.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-pont.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A symmetric power of a variety of dimension <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is always singular, so the symmetric power of a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension at least 2 can not be a Severi–Brauer variety. (See, however, (<a href="#Thmthm37" title="Proposition 37. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>) and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kol18</a>]</cite> for
a birational study of such symmetric powers.)
Instead we have the following construction of larger dimensional
Severi–Brauer varieties from a smaller one.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 17</span></span><span id="Thmthm17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety, and
<math id="Thmthm17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in T\bigl({\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> a vector bundle. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex24.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{F}(P)\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\textstyle{\sum}_{m,n=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl(P,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{F}(P)\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\textstyle{\sum}_{m,n=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl(P,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm17.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm17.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+\operatorname{rank}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+\operatorname{rank}F</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains <math id="Thmthm17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
as a subvariety.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a summand in (<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.1) is nonzero iff <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq n\cdot\operatorname{rank}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq n\cdot\operatorname{rank}F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A conceptual derivation of this formula is given in (<a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>), which also proves that <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{F}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{F}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique Severi–Brauer variety
(up to isomorphism) that contains <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and has dimension <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+\operatorname{rank}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+\operatorname{rank}F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Set <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\operatorname{rank}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\operatorname{rank}F</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to check that if <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{F}(P)\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P+r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{F}(P)\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P+r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F\cong\oplus^{r}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>r</mi></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\oplus^{r}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and
we can rewrite the right hand side of
(<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.1) as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex25.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\sum}_{n=0}^{\infty}\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nr}\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(k^{r})\otimes H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(nr-m)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\sum}_{n=0}^{\infty}\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nr}\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(k^{r})\otimes H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(nr-m)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">A degree <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nr</annotation></semantics></math> homogeneous polynomial in the variables
<math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0},\dots,x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0},\dots,x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},\dots,y_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},\dots,y_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely written as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nr}\textstyle{\sum}_{|I|=m}{\mathbf{y}}^{I}f_{I}({\mathbf{x}})\quad\mbox{where}\quad\deg f_{I}=nr-m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>I</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nr}\textstyle{\sum}_{|I|=m}{\mathbf{y}}^{I}f_{I}({\mathbf{x}})\quad\mbox{where}\quad\deg f_{I}=nr-m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Thus the sum (<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.2) can be naturally identified with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex27.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\sum}_{n=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl({\mathbb{P}}^{N},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{N}}(nr)\bigr)\subset\textstyle{\sum}_{d=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl({\mathbb{P}}^{N},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{N}}(d)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\sum}_{n=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl({\mathbb{P}}^{N},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{N}}(nr)\bigr)\subset\textstyle{\sum}_{d=0}^{\infty}H^{0}\bigl({\mathbb{P}}^{N},{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{N}}(d)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\dim P+r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\dim P+r</annotation></semantics></math>, and two sides of (<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.3) have the same <math id="S2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Proj</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Proj}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Thinking of <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\sum}_{n}H^{0}\bigl(P,(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\sum}_{n}H^{0}\bigl(P,(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of the sum in (<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.1) corresponding to <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
we see that
<math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\textstyle{\sum}_{n}H^{0}\bigl(P,(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>k</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\textstyle{\sum}_{n}H^{0}\bigl(P,(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a subvariety of <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{F}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{F}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If we blow up 6 points in <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a cubic surface, and the same holds for Severi–Brauer surfaces.
One can reverse this construction by first writing down some
cubic surfaces and then proving that they are birational to Severi–Brauer surfaces over a field <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The key step is to describe the lines
on a cubic surface, and the action of the absolute Galois group
<math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{k}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(\bar{k}/k)</annotation></semantics></math> on them.
These tasks are easier for singular cubic surfaces: there are fewer lines and they are easier to locate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Higher dimensional variants of these examples are discussed in Section <a href="#S9" title="9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">18</span></span><span id="Thmthm18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The cubic surface <math id="Thmthm18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:=(xyz=t^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:=(xyz=t^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperplane section <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\cap(t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\cap(t=0)</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of 3 lines <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the surface has <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-singularities at the 3 points where 2 of the lines intersect.
If we resolve these singularities, the birational transforms of the lines <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the 6 exceptional curves form a cycle of smooth rational curves.
We can describe it by the dual graph</p>
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm18" title="18The cubic surface 
            
              
                :=
                S
                
                  
                  
                    (
                    
                      =
                      
                        ⁢
                        x
                        y
                        z
                      
                      
                        
                        t
                        3
                      
                    
                    )
                  
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccccccc}2&amp;-&amp;2&amp;-\quad 1\quad-&amp;2&amp;-&amp;2\\
|&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;|\\
1&amp;-&amp;2&amp;\rule[1.0pt]{40.0pt}{0.4pt}&amp;2&amp;-&amp;1\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo>−</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mtext><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:40.0pt;height:0.4pt;position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></mtext></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccccccc}2&amp;-&amp;2&amp;-\quad 1\quad-&amp;2&amp;-&amp;2\\
|&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;|\\
1&amp;-&amp;2&amp;\rule[1.0pt]{40.0pt}{0.4pt}&amp;2&amp;-&amp;1\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm18.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each curve is represented by minus its self-intersection number, two curves are disjoint if they are not connected by an edge, and meet at single point if they are connected by an edge.
The birational transforms of the lines have self-intersection <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If we contract them we get
the dual graph</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm18" title="18The cubic surface 
            
              
                :=
                S
                
                  
                  
                    (
                    
                      =
                      
                        ⁢
                        x
                        y
                        z
                      
                      
                        
                        t
                        3
                      
                    
                    )
                  
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{ccccc}1&amp;-&amp;1&amp;-&amp;1\\
|&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;|\\
1&amp;-&amp;1&amp;-&amp;1\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>−</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{ccccc}1&amp;-&amp;1&amp;-&amp;1\\
|&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;|\\
1&amp;-&amp;1&amp;-&amp;1\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm18.p1.3" class="ltx_p">There are 2 ways to choose 3 disjoint <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-curves from the above 6. Contracting either one of these triples gives <math id="Thmthm18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We get interesting variants of these surfaces if we replace
<math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xyz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xyz</annotation></semantics></math> with a cubic form that is irreducible over <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, but splits into linear factors over <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. These are the norm forms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 19</span></span><span id="Thmthm19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Norm forms)<span id="Thmthm19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm19.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field, <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> algebraic over <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=\alpha,\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=\alpha,\alpha_{2},\dots,\alpha_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> its conjugates.
Set <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=k(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=k(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\deg(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\deg(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\alpha,\dots,\alpha^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\alpha,\dots,\alpha^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-basis of <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="Thmthm19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">norm form</span> of <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (or of <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math>) is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex30.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm19" title="Definition 19 (Norm forms). ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=\textstyle{\prod}_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(x_{1}+\alpha_{i}x_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}^{n-1}x_{n}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=\textstyle{\prod}_{i=1}^{n}\bigl(x_{1}+\alpha_{i}x_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}^{n-1}x_{n}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm19.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The product is symmetric in the <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus</p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm19" title="Definition 19 (Norm forms). ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})\in k[x_{1},\dots,x_{n}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})\in k[x_{1},\dots,x_{n}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm19.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The notation suppresses the dependence on the basis
<math id="Thmthm19.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\alpha,\dots,\alpha^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\alpha,\dots,\alpha^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using a different basis results in another
norm form that differs by a <math id="Thmthm19.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-linear coordinate change. This will not be important for us.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm19.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm19.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous of degree <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>,
and has only the trivial zero in <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed if
<math id="Thmthm19.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(p_{1},\dots,p_{n})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(p_{1},\dots,p_{n})=0</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="Thmthm19.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}+\alpha_{i}p_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}^{n-1}p_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}+\alpha_{i}p_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}^{n-1}p_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, but
the <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\alpha_{i},\dots,\alpha_{i}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\alpha_{i},\dots,\alpha_{i}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent over <math id="Thmthm19.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm19.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm19.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to work out that if <math id="Thmthm19.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\sqrt[3]{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mroot><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></mroot></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\sqrt[3]{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm19.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=k(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=k(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex32.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm19" title="Definition 19 (Norm forms). ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=x_{1}^{3}+ax_{2}^{3}+a^{2}x_{3}^{3}-3ax_{1}x_{2}x_{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=x_{1}^{3}+ax_{2}^{3}+a^{2}x_{3}^{3}-3ax_{1}x_{2}x_{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20</span></span><span id="Thmthm20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Twisted forms of the cubic surface <math id="Thmthm20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xyz=t^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xyz=t^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field and <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=k(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=k(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> a cubic field extension.
Pick <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in k</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the cubic surface</p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex33.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm20" title="20Twisted forms of the cubic surface 
            
              
                =
                
                  ⁢
                  x
                  y
                  z
                
                
                  
                  t
                  3
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=S(K,d):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(x+\alpha y+\alpha^{2}z)=dt^{3}\bigr)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=S(K,d):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(x+\alpha y+\alpha^{2}z)=dt^{3}\bigr)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm20.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Over <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> it is isomorphic to <math id="Thmthm20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(xyz=t^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(xyz=t^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to work out that if <math id="Thmthm20.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> is Galois, then the
six <math id="Thmthm20.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-curves in (<a href="#Thmthm18" title="18The cubic surface 
            
              
                :=
                S
                
                  
                  
                    (
                    
                      =
                      
                        ⁢
                        x
                        y
                        z
                      
                      
                        
                        t
                        3
                      
                    
                    )
                  
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>.2) form 2 Galois orbits. Contracting either
orbit gives a Severi–Brauer surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(<span id="Thmthm20.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside <a href="#Thmthm20" title="20Twisted forms of the cubic surface 
            
              
                =
                
                  ⁢
                  x
                  y
                  z
                
                
                  
                  t
                  3
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.2.</span> If <math id="Thmthm20.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> is not Galois, then the
six <math id="Thmthm20.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-curves form one Galois orbit, and we have a
degree 6 Del Pezzo surface of Picard number 1 over <math id="Thmthm20.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As a concrete example, we get the following using (<a href="#Thmthm19" title="Definition 19 (Norm forms). ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>.3).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm20.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm20.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm20.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example <a href="#Thmthm20" title="20Twisted forms of the cubic surface 
            
              
                =
                
                  ⁢
                  x
                  y
                  z
                
                
                  
                  t
                  3
                
              
            
          . ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>.3.</span> Let <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field of characteristic <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq 2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq 2,3</annotation></semantics></math> that contains <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,d\in k^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,d\in k^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>
and assume that <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not a norm in <math id="Thmthm20.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\bigl[\sqrt[3]{a}\bigr]/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mroot><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></mroot><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\bigl[\sqrt[3]{a}\bigr]/k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the cubic surface</p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{3}+ay^{3}+a^{2}z^{3}-3axyz=dt^{3})\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{3}+ay^{3}+a^{2}z^{3}-3axyz=dt^{3})\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm20.p5.2" class="ltx_p">is birational to 2 nontrivial Severi–Brauer surfaces. ∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Using similar arguments, Skolem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sko55</a>]</cite> describes all Severi–Brauer surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 21</span></span><span id="Thmthm21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field of <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{char}k\neq 2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>char</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{char}k\neq 2,3</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> a cubic extension with discriminant <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{K/k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{K/k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in K^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in K^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in k</annotation></semantics></math> consider the cubic surface</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.Ex35.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm21" title="Theorem 21. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T(K,\beta,d):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})+\operatorname{trace}_{K/k}(\beta{\mathbf{x}})t^{2}+dt^{3}=0\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>trace</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T(K,\beta,d):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})+\operatorname{trace}_{K/k}(\beta{\mathbf{x}})t^{2}+dt^{3}=0\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that <math id="Thmthm21.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(21.2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is rational over </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> iff </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(k)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(k)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(21.3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{K/k}\cdot\bigl(d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{K/k}\cdot\bigl(d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is not a square in </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is birational to a minimal, degree 6 Del Pezzo surface over </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(21.4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{K/k}\cdot\bigl(d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{K/k}\cdot\bigl(d^{2}+4\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(\beta)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a square in </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is birational to a pair of nontrivial Severi–Brauer surfaces </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(21.5)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every Severi–Brauer surface over </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is obtained this way.
More precisely, </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is obtained from </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(K,\beta,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(K,\beta,d)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some
</span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> iff </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 22</span></span><span id="Thmthm22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The study of birational maps between Del Pezzo surfaces was started by Segre <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seg42</a>]</cite>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KSC04</a>]</cite> for an introduction and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">BSY24</a>]</cite> for recent applications
to the Cremona groups <math id="Thmthm22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Bir}({\mathbb{P}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bir</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Bir}({\mathbb{P}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">An example of a 3-dimensional Severi–Brauer variety is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 23</span></span><span id="Thmthm23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a 3-dimensional quadric hypersurface.
Then <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)</annotation></semantics></math>—the variety parametrizing lines in <math id="Thmthm23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>—is a 3-dimensional Severi–Brauer variety.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, but there are many examples where <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(k)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(k)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, yet <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)(k)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)(k)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, this happens if <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\subset{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\subset{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive and
<math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\bigl(a_{1}x_{1}^{2}+\cdots+a_{4}x_{4}^{2}=a_{0}x_{0}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\bigl(a_{1}x_{1}^{2}+\cdots+a_{4}x_{4}^{2}=a_{0}x_{0}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, since
there are no real lines on an ellipsoid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Sketch of proof. We need to show that <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{3}_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>OG</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{OG}({\mathbb{P}}^{1},Q)_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{3}_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell,\ell^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell,\ell^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote lines on <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that the isomorphism is given by the linear system</p>
<table id="S2.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigl|H_{\ell}:=\{\ell^{\prime}:\ell\cap\ell^{\prime}\neq\emptyset\}\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|H_{\ell}:=\{\ell^{\prime}:\ell\cap\ell^{\prime}\neq\emptyset\}\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.2" class="ltx_p">The key property is to check that <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{\ell}^{3})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{\ell}^{3})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently,
given 3 disjoint lines <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\subset Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1},\ell_{2},\ell_{3}\subset Q</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a unique line
<math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}\subset Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}\subset Q</annotation></semantics></math> that meets all 3. To see this note that
<math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=Q\cap\langle\ell_{1},\ell_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=Q\cap\langle\ell_{1},\ell_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a quadric surface
and <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\ell_{3}\cap\langle\ell_{1},\ell_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\ell_{3}\cap\langle\ell_{1},\ell_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a point on <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is thus a unique line <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that passes through <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and
meets <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1},\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1},\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Twisted linear subvarieties</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we study
Severi–Brauer varieties using their twisted linear subvarieties.
First we show in (<a href="#Thmthm29" title="Definition–Theorem 29. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) that twisted linear subvarieties of a Severi–Brauer variety behave very much like linear subspaces of a vector space.
Then we show another way to associate a skew field to a
Severi–Brauer variety.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 24</span></span><span id="Thmthm24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A subscheme <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> of a
Severi–Brauer variety is called <span id="Thmthm24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted linear</span> if
<math id="Thmthm24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace of <math id="Thmthm24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm24.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}\subset\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}\subset\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the separable closure. Then
<math id="Thmthm24.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm35" title="Lemma 35. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>),
hence, if <math id="Thmthm24.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace, then so is
<math id="Thmthm24.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, since they are both given by the linear forms in the homogeneous ideal of <math id="Thmthm24.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="Thmthm24.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> is twisted linear iff
<math id="Thmthm24.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace of <math id="Thmthm24.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="Thmthm24.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted linear span</span> of a closed subscheme <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset P</annotation></semantics></math>
is denoted by <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that
<math id="Thmthm24.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z\rangle_{\bar{k}}=\langle Z_{\bar{k}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z\rangle_{\bar{k}}=\langle Z_{\bar{k}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>
which is equivalent to <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}=\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}=\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace that contains
<math id="Thmthm24.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}\supset\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z\rangle_{k^{s}}\supset\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, we need to show that <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is
defined over <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clearly invariant under <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus
<math id="Thmthm24.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Z_{k^{s}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="Thmthm24.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#Thmthm25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All twisted linear subspaces form a <span id="Thmthm24.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lattice</span>
<math id="Thmthm24.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> with intersection and span as the lattice operations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A map <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:P\dasharrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:P\dasharrow Q</annotation></semantics></math> between Severi–Brauer varieties is called <span id="Thmthm24.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted linear</span> if it is linear over <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, the composite</p>
<table id="S3.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{\bar{k}}^{\dim P}\cong P_{\bar{k}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\dasharrow}}Q_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{\bar{k}}^{\dim Q}\quad\mbox{is linear.}\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>g</mi></mover><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>is linear.</mtext></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{\bar{k}}^{\dim P}\cong P_{\bar{k}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle g}}{{\dasharrow}}Q_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{\bar{k}}^{\dim Q}\quad\mbox{is linear.}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm24.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The key examples of twisted linear maps are projections.
Let <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear subspace. The projection of <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
with center <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the map <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> given by
those sections of <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> that vanish along <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\perp}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\perp}\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear subspace complementary to <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>,
then we can think of <math id="Thmthm24.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> as a map
<math id="Thmthm24.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{L}:{\mathbb{P}}^{n}\dasharrow L^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{L}:{\mathbb{P}}^{n}\dasharrow L^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next let <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety and
<math id="Thmthm24.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> a twisted linear subvariety. Although
<math id="Thmthm24.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> does not exist, <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)</annotation></semantics></math> does and
we can define the <span id="Thmthm24.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projection</span>
<math id="Thmthm24.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}:P\dasharrow R^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}:P\dasharrow R^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> with <span id="Thmthm24.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">center</span> <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> as the map
given by
those sections of <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)</annotation></semantics></math> that vanish along <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> with multiplicity <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math>. It is clear that <math id="Thmthm24.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a Severi–Brauer variety.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm24.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm24.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We say that two twisted linear subvarieties <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>
are <span id="Thmthm24.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complementary</span> if <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\cap R=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\cap R=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm24.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q,R\rangle=P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q,R\rangle=P</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, if <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\cap R=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\cap R=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm24.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q+\dim R=\dim P-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q+\dim R=\dim P-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> restricts to an isomorphism <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\cong R^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\cong R^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. I particular,
complements of <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> are all twisted linearly isomorphic to each other.
The projection <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}:P\dasharrow R^{\perp}\cong Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}:P\dasharrow R^{\perp}\cong Q</annotation></semantics></math> has a simple geometric description:
a point <math id="Thmthm24.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P\setminus R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P\setminus R</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to
<math id="Thmthm24.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}(p)=Q\cap\langle R,p\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}(p)=Q\cap\langle R,p\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.
We see in (<a href="#Thmthm27" title="Corollary 27. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) that complements exist.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have used
Weil’s lemma on the field of definition of a subscheme;
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei62</a>, I.7.Lem.2]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KSC04</a>, Sec.3.4]</cite> for proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 25</span></span><span id="Thmthm25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> a Galois extension and <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{K}\subset X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{K}\subset X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> a closed subscheme that is invariant under <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. That is,
there is a unique closed subscheme <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z_{k})_{K}=Z_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z_{k})_{K}=Z_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 26</span></span><span id="Thmthm26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Châtelet correspondence)<span id="Thmthm26.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety. Then there are natural correspondences between</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(26.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">direct summands of </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(26.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">left ideals of </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(26.3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted linear subvarieties of </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm26.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm26.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map between the direct summands and the twisted linear subvarieties preserves the lattice structure, that is, intersections, linear spans and complements.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. The equivalence between left ideals <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> and
direct summands <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset F(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, given by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex38.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm26" title="Theorem 26 (Châtelet correspondence). ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I\mapsto G(I):=\cap_{A\in I}\ker A,\quad\mbox{and}\quad G\mapsto I(G):=\{A:G\subset\ker A\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ker</mi><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ker</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\mapsto G(I):=\cap_{A\in I}\ker A,\quad\mbox{and}\quad G\mapsto I(G):=\{A:G\subset\ker A\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">was already discussed in (<a href="#Thmthm10" title="Corollary 10. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To go from direct summands <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> to
twisted linear subvarieties consider
<math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.4). Set</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G):=\mbox{zero locus of the section }{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to F(P)/G." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>zero locus of the section </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G):=\mbox{zero locus of the section }{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to F(P)/G.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(F(P)/G\bigr)_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(F(P)/G\bigr)_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of copies of <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is a common zero locus of some linear polynomials, thus a
twisted linear subvariety.
This <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\mapsto L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\mapsto L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is a bijection over <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence invertible and defined over <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the inverse will be clearer once we write the Euler sequence in an invariant way (<a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>.4). Restriction gives a surjection
<math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}\bigl(P_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{O}}(1)\bigr)\twoheadrightarrow H^{0}\bigl(L_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{O}}(1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}\bigl(P_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{O}}(1)\bigr)\twoheadrightarrow H^{0}\bigl(L_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{O}}(1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>. Its dual is a subspace of <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>.4), hence a
direct summand of <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 27</span></span><span id="Thmthm27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(27.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The minimal twisted linear subvarieties span </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(27.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every twisted linear subvariety </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a complement.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(27.3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\neq Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\neq Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be minimal twisted linear subvarieties. Then their span
</span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains at least one more minimal twisted linear subvariety
</span><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{12}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. We can write <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F(P)\cong\oplus_{m}E_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)\cong\oplus_{m}E_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{m}\cong E(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{m}\cong E(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{m}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{m}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal twisted linear subvariety corresponding to <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> span <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(Q)\subset F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(Q)\subset F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> be the summand corresponding to <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#Thmthm8" title="Lemma 8. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) it has complement <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)=F(Q)\oplus F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)=F(Q)\oplus F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}:=L(F^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}:=L(F^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is a complement of <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the summands that correspond to <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Any isomorphism <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:E_{1}\cong E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:E_{1}\cong E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a summand
<math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}+E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}+E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(E_{\phi})\subset\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(E_{\phi})\subset\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> works.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 28</span></span><span id="Thmthm28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm28.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is
called <span id="Thmthm28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimal</span> if the following
equivalent conditions hold.</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(28.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The only twisted linear subvariety of <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> itself.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(28.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is indecomposable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(28.3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew field.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm28.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm28.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The equivalence of these follows from (<a href="#Thmthm26" title="Theorem 26 (Châtelet correspondence). ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defnthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition–Theorem 29</span></span><span id="Thmthm29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm29.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety. Then all minimal (with respect to inclusion) twisted linear subvarieties
are twisted linear isomorphic. Their isomorphism class is denoted by <math id="Thmthm29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm29.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm29.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To see the isomorphisms, let <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},Q_{2}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},Q_{2}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be
minimal (with respect to inclusion) twisted linear subvarieties.
Let <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be a complement to <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm29.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{12}\subset\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{12}\subset\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> be a common complement to the <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{1},Q_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{12},R\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{12},R\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is a
common complement to the <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. As we noted in (<a href="#Thmthm24" title="Definition 24. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), both <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic to <math id="Thmthm29.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q_{12},R\rangle^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q_{12},R\rangle^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside on the Fundamental Theorem of Projective Geometry</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 30</span></span><span id="Thmthm30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm30.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An abstract <span id="Thmthm30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective space</span> <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}:=(P,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}:=(P,L)</annotation></semantics></math>
consists of a set <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
(called points) and a set <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> of subsets of <math id="Thmthm30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (called lines) satisfying the following axioms.</p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(30.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any two distinct points <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},p_{2}\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},p_{2}\in P</annotation></semantics></math> there is a unique line containing them. We denote this line by <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(30.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be 4 distinct points. If
<math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\cap\langle p_{3},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\cap\langle p_{3},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>
then also <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p_{1},p_{3}\rangle\cap\langle p_{2},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p_{1},p_{3}\rangle\cap\langle p_{2},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(30.3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every line has at least 3 points.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm30.p1.2" class="ltx_p">(<span id="Thmthm30.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comment.</span> The aim of the definition of abstract projective spaces is to axiomatize the properties of linear subspaces of projective spaces over fields. This leads to a long list of axioms but it turns out that everything can be derived from the above 3.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm30.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm30.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A subset <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="Thmthm30.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear subspace</span> if
<math id="Thmthm30.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},p_{2}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},p_{2}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="Thmthm30.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension</span> of <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the supremum of the lengths of chains of linear subspaces</p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset\subsetneq M_{1}\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_{d}\subsetneq P." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊊</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊊</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>⊊</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset\subsetneq M_{1}\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq M_{d}\subsetneq P.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm30.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathbf{P}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathbf{P}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P|=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm30.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathbf{P}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathbf{P}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmthm30.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|L|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|L|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm30.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm30.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Four points <math id="Thmthm30.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="Thmthm30.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">coplanar</span> if
<math id="Thmthm30.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\cap\langle p_{3},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p_{1},p_{2}\rangle\cap\langle p_{3},p_{4}\rangle\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus axiom (<a href="#Thmthm30" title="Definition 30. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>.2) says that this does not depend on the order of the 4 points.
It is clear that <math id="Thmthm30.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathbf{P}}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>𝐏</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathbf{P}}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> iff any 4 points are coplanar.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 31</span></span><span id="Thmthm31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm31.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a skew field and <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space.
We denote by <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> the projective space consisting of the sets</p>
<table id="S3.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcl}P&amp;:=&amp;\{\mbox{1-dimensional subspaces of $V$}\}\quad\mbox{and}\\
L&amp;:=&amp;\{\mbox{2-dimensional subspaces of $V$}\}.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>P</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>1-dimensional subspaces of </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>L</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>2-dimensional subspaces of </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcl}P&amp;:=&amp;\{\mbox{1-dimensional subspaces of $V$}\}\quad\mbox{and}\\
L&amp;:=&amp;\{\mbox{2-dimensional subspaces of $V$}\}.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm31.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To be precise, a line is the set of all 1-dimensional subspaces
contained in a given 2-dimensional subspace.
It is easy to check that <math id="Thmthm31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> is an abstract projective space.
(Note that this is the dual of the Grothendieck convention that is
common in algebraic geometry.)</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamental theorem says that
(<a href="#Thmthm31" title="Example 31. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) gives all projective spaces of dimension <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theorem is due to von Staudt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">vS1857</a>]</cite>, but
the first complete proofs are in
Reye’s lectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rey1866</a>]</cite> (starting with the second edition), using a correction by Klein <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kle1874</a>]</cite>.
Improved versions were given by Russell <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rus1903</a>]</cite>,
Whitehead <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Whi1906</a>]</cite>,
Veblen and Young <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">VY1908</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 32</span></span><span id="Thmthm32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fundamental Theorem of Projective Geometry)<span id="Thmthm32.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a projective space of dimension <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a unique skew field <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm32.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{P}}\cong{\mathcal{P}}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{P}}\cong{\mathcal{P}}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cox64</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cam92</a>]</cite> for introductions, and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">HP47</a>, Chap.VI]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bae52</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Art57</a>]</cite>, or
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas06</a>, Chap.5]</cite>
for full treatments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this to the lattice <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 33</span></span><span id="Thmthm33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety over a field <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> the lattice of its twisted linear subspaces.
Assume that <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{L}}(P)\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{L}}(P)\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a unique skew field <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, the lattice of all linear subspaces of <math id="Thmthm33.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Note that (<a href="#Thmthm33" title="Corollary 33. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) also holds if <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim{\mathcal{L}}(P)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim{\mathcal{L}}(P)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, but
there are abstract
projective planes that do not come from skew fields; see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cas06</a>, Chap.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Splitting fields</h2>

<div id="Thmthm34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 34</span></span><span id="Thmthm34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm34.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
A field extension <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="Thmthm34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">splitting field</span> of <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> iff
<math id="Thmthm34.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point. By (<a href="#Thmthm14" title="Corollary 14 (Châtelet’s theorem). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) this is equivalent to <math id="Thmthm34.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{K}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{K}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a splitting field, but there are much smaller ones.
We start with a weak but useful claim, and then prove the optimal result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 35</span></span><span id="Thmthm35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every Severi–Brauer variety has a separable splitting field.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. As we discuss in (<a href="#Thmthm38" title="Aside 38 (Separable points). ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>), every smooth variety
has a separable point. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 36</span></span><span id="Thmthm36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety over an infinite field <math id="Thmthm36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(36.1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The zero-set of a general section of </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a smooth, 0-dimensional subscheme of degree </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(36.2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a separable splitting field of degree </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Since <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math>, we may as well assume that <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fix coordinates <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0},\dots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0},\dots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The tangent bundle of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
admits a presentation</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S4.Ex42.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm36" title="Theorem 36. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mover><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>e</mi></mover><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Now choose different <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\in k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\in k</annotation></semantics></math> and
consider the section <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{0}x_{0},\dots,c_{n}x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{0}x_{0},\dots,c_{n}x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{i=0}^{n}{\mathcal{O}}_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in H^{0}({\mathbb{P}}^{n},T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}})</annotation></semantics></math> be its image.
Then <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s=0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth subscheme consisting of the coordinate vertices.
Thus the zero-set of a general section of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{\mathbb{P}}^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth, 0-dimensional subscheme <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite, the same holds for
<math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset\neq Z^{\prime}\subset Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset\neq Z^{\prime}\subset Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a subscheme.
Since <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, the twisted linear span of <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must equal <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg Z^{\prime}\geq n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg Z^{\prime}\geq n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible (over <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>).
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside <a href="#Thmthm36" title="Theorem 36. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>.4.</span> The algebraic variant of this claim is usually
proved using the reduced norm as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>, 4.5.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Another use of <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(P,T_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P,T_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 37</span></span><span id="Thmthm37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmthm37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">KS04</a><span id="Thmthm37.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="Thmthm37.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> 
Let <math id="Thmthm37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The following proof is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kol18</a>, 8]</cite>. Sending a section of <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> to its zero set
gives a rational map <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:H^{0}(P,T_{P})\dasharrow\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:H^{0}(P,T_{P})\dasharrow\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be a reduced 0-cycle of degree <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> whose linear span equals <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is the linear space
<math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}\bigl(P,T_{P}(-Z)\bigr)\subset H^{0}(P,T_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}\bigl(P,T_{P}(-Z)\bigr)\subset H^{0}(P,T_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset H^{0}(P,T_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset H^{0}(P,T_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> be a general affine-linear subspace of codimension
<math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{V}:V\dasharrow\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{V}:V\dasharrow\operatorname{Sym}^{n+1}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is birational. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 38</span></span><span id="Thmthm38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Separable points)<span id="Thmthm38.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every <math id="Thmthm38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> variety has points in a finite extension <math id="Thmthm38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}/k</annotation></semantics></math>.
In positive characteristic it is frequently very useful to have points in
a finite and separable extension.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are at least 3 equivalent versions of the existence of separable points. The first variant is called
the Separating transcendence basis theorem, proved by MacLane in 1939;
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei62</a>, p.18]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jac80</a>, 8.37]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lan02</a>, Sec.VIII.4]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eis95</a>, p.558]</cite>
for algebraic proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm38.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theorem <a href="#Thmthm38" title="Aside 38 (Separable points). ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>.1.</span> Let <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> be a finitely generated field extension of characteristic <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and of transcendence degree <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\otimes_{k}k^{1/p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\otimes_{k}k^{1/p}</annotation></semantics></math> has no nilpotents (hence, in fact, it is a field). Then there is a sub-extension <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\supset k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\supset k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\supset k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\supset k</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="Thmthm38.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\supset k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\supset k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, separable, and
<math id="Thmthm38.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\supset k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\supset k</annotation></semantics></math> is
purely transcendental.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm38.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theorem <a href="#Thmthm38" title="Aside 38 (Separable points). ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>.2.</span>
Let <math id="Thmthm38.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a geometrically reduced <math id="Thmthm38.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm38.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there is a generically finite and separable map <math id="Thmthm38.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(There are sharper variants of this.
If <math id="Thmthm38.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is projective, one can choose <math id="Thmthm38.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be finite and separable;
if <math id="Thmthm38.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is affine one can choose <math id="Thmthm38.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to{\mathbb{A}}^{n}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be finite and separable.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eis95</a>, 16.18]</cite> for the latter version.)</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm38.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm38.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm38.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theorem <a href="#Thmthm38" title="Aside 38 (Separable points). ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>.3.</span> A <math id="Thmthm38.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety
<math id="Thmthm38.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is geometrically reduced iff
<math id="Thmthm38.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a smooth point over a finite and separable extension of <math id="Thmthm38.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All Severi–Brauer varieties are built up from
minimal Severi–Brauer varieties in a transparent way.
The key to this is the following result, which shows how to
construct a Severi–Brauer variety from a
twisted linear subvariety.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 39</span></span><span id="Thmthm39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety and
<math id="Thmthm39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> a twisted linear subvariety. Let
<math id="Thmthm39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:P\dasharrow R:=Q^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:P\dasharrow R:=Q^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding projection, and
<math id="Thmthm39.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:B_{Q}P\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:B_{Q}P\to P</annotation></semantics></math> the blow-up of <math id="Thmthm39.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following hold.</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(39.1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The composite </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\pi}:=\pi\circ\sigma:B_{Q}P\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\pi}:=\pi\circ\sigma:B_{Q}P\to R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a
</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-bundle.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(39.2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a vector bundle </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in T(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in T(R)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that
</span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{R}\bigl(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{R}\bigl(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(39.3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is uniquely determined by </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(39.4)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is birational to </span><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\times{\mathbb{P}}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\times{\mathbb{P}}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\dim P-\dim Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\dim P-\dim Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset B_{Q}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset B_{Q}P</annotation></semantics></math> be the exceptional divisor.
A geometric fiber of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear space <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\bar{k}}\subset L\subset P_{\bar{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\bar{k}}\subset L\subset P_{\bar{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\dim Q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\dim Q+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\cap L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\cap L</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Pushing forward the injection <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}\subset{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}\subset{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> we get</p>
<table id="S5.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex43.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{R}\to\tilde{\pi}_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)\to F^{*}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{R}\to\tilde{\pi}_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)\to F^{*}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\dim Q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\dim Q+1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Working over <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> shows that
<math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{*}_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(-1)^{\oplus\dim Q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}_{\bar{k}}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(-1)^{\oplus\dim Q+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
(<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>.2) holds and (<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>.5) splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rank}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}F</annotation></semantics></math> determine <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P</annotation></semantics></math>. There are many ways to see that they also determine <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. We give an explicit formula for <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>), but the simplest is to note that <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P</annotation></semantics></math> has Picard number 2, hence at most 2 nontrivial contractions. One of them is
<math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P\to R</annotation></semantics></math> the other is <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P\to P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P\to P</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{Q}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{Q}P</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely determines <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally (<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>.4) follows from (<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>.2). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40</span></span><span id="Thmthm40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit description of <math id="Thmthm40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex44.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{\bar{k}}}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle,
but the other 2 are twisted line bundles.
Setting <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:=\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:=\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we get an isomorphism of line bundles over <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S5.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex45.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(c)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(cE)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(c)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(cE)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, for every <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> we get that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex46.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{rcl}H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(dc)\bigr)&amp;=&amp;H^{0}\bigl(B_{Q}R,{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(dcE)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(dc)\bigr)\\[4.30554pt]
&amp;=&amp;H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{dc}(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R})\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(dc)\bigr).\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{rcl}H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(dc)\bigr)&amp;=&amp;H^{0}\bigl(B_{Q}R,{\mathcal{O}}_{B_{Q}P}(dcE)\otimes\tilde{\pi}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(dc)\bigr)\\[4.30554pt]
&amp;=&amp;H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{dc}(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R})\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{R}(dc)\bigr).\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Adding these up for all <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the left hand side becomes
<math id="Thmthm40.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\sum}_{d}H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(dc)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>d</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\sum}_{d}H^{0}\bigl(P,{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(dc)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose proj is <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=\dim Q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=\dim Q+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Adding up the right hand side for those <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q|d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q|d</annotation></semantics></math>,
and setting <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=dc/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=dc/q</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get</p>
<table id="S5.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex47.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{c|n}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{nq}(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{c|n}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{nq}(F^{*}+{\mathcal{O}}_{R})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.5" class="ltx_p">The proj does not change if we let the summation run over all <math id="Thmthm40.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Expanding the symmetric powers now gives that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex48.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{n\geq 0}\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nq}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{n\geq 0}\textstyle{\sum}_{m=0}^{nq}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm40.p1.6" class="ltx_p">We already noted in (<a href="#Thmthm17" title="Theorem 17. ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) that this is
equivalent to</p>
<table id="S5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm40" title="40Explicit description of 
            
              P
            
          . ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{n,m\geq 0}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Sym</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\operatorname{Proj}_{k}\Bigl(\textstyle{\sum}_{n,m\geq 0}H^{0}\bigl(R,\operatorname{Sym}^{m}(F^{*})\otimes(\det F)^{\otimes n}\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm41" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm41.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 41</span></span><span id="Thmthm41.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Flag transitivity)<span id="Thmthm41.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm41.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm41.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm41.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm41.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two Severi–Brauer varieties
such that <math id="Thmthm41.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset\subsetneq Q^{i}_{1}\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq Q^{i}_{m_{i}}\subsetneq P_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>⊊</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">⊊</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⊊</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset\subsetneq Q^{i}_{1}\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq Q^{i}_{m_{i}}\subsetneq P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm41.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm41.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be two maximal chains of twisted linear subvarieties. Assume that
either <math id="Thmthm41.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P_{1}\leq\dim P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P_{1}\leq\dim P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="Thmthm41.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}\leq m_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}\leq m_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a twisted linear embedding <math id="Thmthm41.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:P_{1}\lhook\joinrel\to P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⸦→</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:P_{1}\lhook\joinrel\to P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm41.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(Q^{1}_{j})=Q^{2}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(Q^{1}_{j})=Q^{2}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmthm41.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq m_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq m_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Proof.
We already know that all minimal twisted linear subvarieties
have the same dimension <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i(P)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i(P)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#Thmthm29" title="Definition–Theorem 29. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>),
<math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{i}_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{i}_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a minimal twisted linear subvariety <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that is disjoint from <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</p>
<table id="S5.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{i}_{j}\subsetneq\langle Q^{i}_{j},R^{i}_{j}\rangle\subset Q^{i}_{j+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>⊊</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{i}_{j}\subsetneq\langle Q^{i}_{j},R^{i}_{j}\rangle\subset Q^{i}_{j+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p">By the maximality of the chains <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle Q^{i}_{j},R^{i}_{j}\rangle=Q^{i}_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle Q^{i}_{j},R^{i}_{j}\rangle=Q^{i}_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q^{i}_{j+1}=\dim Q^{i}_{j}+i(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q^{i}_{j+1}=\dim Q^{i}_{j}+i(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. This shows that
<math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim Q^{i}_{j}=j\cdot i(P)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim Q^{i}_{j}=j\cdot i(P)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the 2 assumptions
<math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\dim P_{1}\leq\dim P_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\dim P_{1}\leq\dim P_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m_{1}\leq m_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m_{1}\leq m_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent.
After replacing <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{2}_{m_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{2}_{m_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> we may assume that
<math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=m_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=m_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; call this common value <math id="S5.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Next we use induction on <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}=P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}=P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we are done.
For <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we may assume that there is a twisted linear isomorphism <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m}:Q^{1}_{m}\cong Q^{2}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m}:Q^{1}_{m}\cong Q^{2}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m}(Q^{1}_{j})=Q^{2}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m}(Q^{1}_{j})=Q^{2}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>), <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{i}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{i}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely determine <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a twisted linear isomorphism <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:P_{1}\cong P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:P_{1}\cong P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to define Brauer equivalence of
Severi–Brauer varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm42" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defnlem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm42.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition–Lemma 42</span></span><span id="Thmthm42.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm42.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm42.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that two Severi–Brauer varieties <math id="Thmthm42.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are
<span id="Thmthm42.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">similar</span> or <span id="Thmthm42.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Brauer-equivalent,</span>
denoted by <math id="Thmthm42.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\sim P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\sim P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, iff the following equivalent conditions hold.</p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(42.1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>min</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{\rm min}\cong P_{2}^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(42.2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The smaller dimensional one is
isomorphic to a twisted linear subvariety of the other.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(42.3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a twisted linear map from the larger
dimensional one onto the other.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(42.4)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a twisted linear map <math id="S5.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:P_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:P_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. We need to check that the four variants are equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P_{2}\geq\dim P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P_{2}\geq\dim P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.1) holds then (<a href="#Thmthm41" title="Theorem 41 (Flag transitivity). ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) shows that
<math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a twisted linear subvariety of <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Projecting from a complement of <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\subset P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\subset P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a
twisted linear map from <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
(<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.1) <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.2) <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.3) while
(<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.2) <math id="S5.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.4)
and (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.3) <math id="S5.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.4) are both clear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Finally assume (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.4). Let <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\subset P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the locus of indeterminacy of <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
and let <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{m}\subset P_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{m}\subset P_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimal twisted linear subvariety
contained in a complement of <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(P_{1}^{m})\subset P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(P_{1}^{m})\subset P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a minimal twisted linear subvariety that is isomorphic to <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.4) <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.1). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm43" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm43.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 43</span></span><span id="Thmthm43.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm43.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm43.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety and
<math id="Thmthm43.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q,R\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q,R\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> complementary twisted linear subvarieties
with normal bundles <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{Q,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{Q,P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{R,P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{R,P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We have interesting isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S5.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex52.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm43" title="Aside 43. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{Q}\bigl(N_{Q,P}^{*}\bigr)\cong Q\times R\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{R}\bigl(N_{R,P}^{*}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{Q}\bigl(N_{Q,P}^{*}\bigr)\cong Q\times R\cong{\mathbb{P}}_{R}\bigl(N_{R,P}^{*}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm43.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To see this, let <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in Q</annotation></semantics></math> be a point and <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{q}\ni q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∋</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{q}\ni q</annotation></semantics></math> a normal line to <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
The projection of <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{Q}\bigl(N_{Q,P}^{*}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{Q}\bigl(N_{Q,P}^{*}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex53.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm43" title="Aside 43. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}:(q,\ell_{q})\mapsto\langle Q,\ell_{q}\rangle\cap R." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}:(q,\ell_{q})\mapsto\langle Q,\ell_{q}\rangle\cap R.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm43.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus the coordinate projection <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Q}:Q\times R\to Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Q}:Q\times R\to Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a
non-trivial <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim R}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle, and <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{R}:Q\times R\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{R}:Q\times R\to R</annotation></semantics></math> is a
non-trivial <math id="Thmthm43.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm43.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm43.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note also that while <math id="Thmthm43.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{Q_{\bar{k}}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{Q_{\bar{k}}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm43.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> are twisted line bundles,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S5.Ex54.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm43" title="Aside 43. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\cong q^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q_{\bar{k}}}(1)\otimes r^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>Q</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\cong q^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q_{\bar{k}}}(1)\otimes r^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{R_{\bar{k}}}(-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm43.p2.2" class="ltx_p">is an actual line bundle. A similar formula leads to the definition of the dual
Severi–Brauer variety in (<a href="#Thmthm47" title="Definition 47. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Twisted linear systems</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|L|:={\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X,L)^{\vee}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|L|:={\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X,L)^{\vee}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is the
<span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear system</span> associated to <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted line bundle on <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then
there does not seem to be any way of defining <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(X,{\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(X,{\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, one can define the
<span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted linear system</span> <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>; it is a Severi–Brauer variety. We discuss three ways of constructing
<math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math>. The first—most general one—is equivalent to
viewing a Severi–Brauer variety as a Galois cohomology class.
The second relies on the existence of the Hilbert scheme.
The third approach is quite elementary but it does not give the most general result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm44" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm44.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 44</span></span><span id="Thmthm44.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(First construction)<span id="Thmthm44.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm44.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm44.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> a
simple, coherent sheaf
on <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As we noted in Aside <a href="#Thmthm7" title="Aside 7. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, this holds
if <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}={\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}={\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted line bundle on <math id="Thmthm44.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm44.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm44.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="Thmthm44.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
simple, the isomorphism <math id="Thmthm44.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is unique up-to a multiplicative scalar, so we get well-defined
isomorphisms</p>
<table id="S6.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\sigma}:{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma})^{\vee}\bigr)\cong{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}})^{\vee}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\sigma}:{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}}^{\sigma})^{\vee}\bigr)\cong{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}})^{\vee}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm44.p2.2" class="ltx_p">These define a <math id="Thmthm44.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme structure on
<math id="Thmthm44.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}})^{\vee}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(X_{k^{s}},{\mathcal{G}})^{\vee}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm45" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm45.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">45</span></span><span id="Thmthm45.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second construction.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm45.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm45.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper, geometrically connected and normal <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme. Let <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a line bundle
on <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{\sigma}\cong{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmthm45.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm45.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm45.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm45.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the
irreducible component of the Hilbert scheme of <math id="Thmthm45.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
parametrizing subschemes <math id="Thmthm45.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm45.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the linear system <math id="Thmthm45.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm45.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm45.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By our assumption <math id="Thmthm45.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under the
Galois group <math id="Thmthm45.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(k^{s}/k)</annotation></semantics></math>, so it is naturally a <math id="Thmthm45.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety
by (<a href="#Thmthm25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3. Twisted linear subvarieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), hence
a Severi–Brauer variety.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm46" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm46.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">46</span></span><span id="Thmthm46.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Third construction using Veronese embeddings.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm46.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm46.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a
<math id="Thmthm46.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme, and <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fix a natural number <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Sending <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in H^{0}(Y,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in H^{0}(Y,M)</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="Thmthm46.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{e}\in H^{0}(Y,M^{e})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{e}\in H^{0}(Y,M^{e})</annotation></semantics></math> descends to an embedding</p>
<table id="S6.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex56.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{e,M}:|M|\lhook\joinrel\to|M^{e}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{e,M}:|M|\lhook\joinrel\to|M^{e}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm46.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We apply this to <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=X_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=X_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmthm46.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:={\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:={\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
there is a line bundle <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{(e)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{(e)}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="Thmthm46.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L^{(e)})_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L^{(e)})_{k^{s}}\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(We can take <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\operatorname{rank}E({\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> by Remark <a href="#Thmthm9" title="Remark 9. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.) Then</p>
<table id="S6.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex57.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{e,{\mathcal{L}}}:|{\mathcal{L}}|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{L}}^{e}|\cong|L^{(e)}|_{k^{s}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{e,{\mathcal{L}}}:|{\mathcal{L}}|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{L}}^{e}|\cong|L^{(e)}|_{k^{s}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm46.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We claim that the image of <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{e,{\mathcal{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{e,{\mathcal{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math>—which, by construction, is defined by equations over <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>—can be defined by equations over <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
If this holds then the resulting <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety is <math id="Thmthm46.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm46.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm46.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We leave the general setting to the reader, but
discuss <math id="Thmthm46.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{k^{s}}}(1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(P,{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{O}}_{P_{k^{s}}}(1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>;
the case that we need later.
We rely on the following simple observation:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm46.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm46.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A hypersurface <math id="Thmthm46.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm46.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="Thmthm46.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>-fold hyperplane iff every point of it has multiplicity <math id="Thmthm46.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm46.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm46.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>. We now that <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{(n+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)\cong{\mathcal{L}}^{(n+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the universal hypersurface with projection</p>
<table id="S6.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex58.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{H}}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|\times P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{H}}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|\times P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\pi}}{{\to}}|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm46.p4.2" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see that <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,x)\mapsto\operatorname{mult}_{x}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>mult</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,x)\mapsto\operatorname{mult}_{x}H</annotation></semantics></math> is an upper semi-continuous function on <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for every <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> there is a closed <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-subscheme
<math id="Thmthm46.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{c}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{c}\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n+1)|</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing those hypersurfaces <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{mult}_{x}H\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>mult</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{mult}_{x}H\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in H</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the construction in (<a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>), since</p>
<table id="S6.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex59.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{n+1,{\mathcal{L}}}\bigl(|{\mathcal{L}}|\bigr)=(W_{n+1})_{k^{s}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{n+1,{\mathcal{L}}}\bigl(|{\mathcal{L}}|\bigr)=(W_{n+1})_{k^{s}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm46.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|:=W_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|:=W_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sought after <math id="Thmthm46.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-structure in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm46.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm46.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that this method works for
<math id="Thmthm46.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,{\mathcal{L}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,{\mathcal{L}})</annotation></semantics></math> if every member of <math id="Thmthm46.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> is generically reduced and <math id="Thmthm46.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}(X_{k^{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}(X_{k^{s}})</annotation></semantics></math> is torsion free.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This special case already allows us to define duals.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm47" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm47.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 47</span></span><span id="Thmthm47.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm47.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm47.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="Thmthm47.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dual</span> of a Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm47.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="Thmthm47.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}:=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}:=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="Thmthm47.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Severi–Brauer variety that
parametrizes all hyperplanes in <math id="Thmthm47.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm47.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm47.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is a natural isomorphism</p>
<table id="S6.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex60.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm47" title="Definition 47. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathcal{P}}:P\cong(P^{\vee})^{\vee}=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(1)|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>given by</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathcal{P}}:P\cong(P^{\vee})^{\vee}=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(1)|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm47.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm47.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\mapsto P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\mapsto P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is a duality for Severi–Brauer varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm47.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm47.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is important to note that the incidence divisor
<math id="Thmthm47.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(x,H):x\in H\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(x,H):x\in H\}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmthm47.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="Thmthm47.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="Thmthm47.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a (non–twisted) line bundle on <math id="Thmthm47.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm48" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm48.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 48</span></span><span id="Thmthm48.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm48.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm48.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a line bundle on <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm48.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:{\mathcal{O}}_{X}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> a section that does not vanish on any irreducible component of <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then tensoring with <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S6.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(s):E({\mathcal{L}})\to E({\mathcal{L}}\otimes M)\quad\mbox{and}\quad s^{(e)}:L^{(e)}\to(L\otimes M)^{(e)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(s):E({\mathcal{L}})\to E({\mathcal{L}}\otimes M)\quad\mbox{and}\quad s^{(e)}:L^{(e)}\to(L\otimes M)^{(e)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm48.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(e)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(e)}</annotation></semantics></math> descends to a
twisted linear map</p>
<table id="S6.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{s}:|{\mathcal{L}}|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{L}}\otimes M|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{s}:|{\mathcal{L}}|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{L}}\otimes M|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm48.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="Thmthm48.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}\otimes M|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}\otimes M|</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm49" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm49.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 49</span></span><span id="Thmthm49.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm49.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm49.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="Thmthm49.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies one of the assumptions in
(<a href="#Thmthm44" title="Aside 44 (First construction). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>–<a href="#Thmthm46" title="46Third construction using Veronese embeddings. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>)
and let <math id="Thmthm49.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
twisted line bundle on <math id="Thmthm49.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that is generated by global sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm49.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm49.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As in (<a href="#Thmthm47" title="Definition 47. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>.1) there is a natural morphism</p>
<table id="S6.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex63.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\to|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>given by</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\to|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm49.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}}(1)\cong{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}}(1)\cong{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence, by (<a href="#Thmthm12" title="Corollary 12. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S6.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex64.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\mathcal{L}})=\iota_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}E\bigl(|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\mathcal{L}})=\iota_{\mathcal{L}}^{*}E\bigl(|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm49.p2.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not
base point free, but <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth, one can still define a rational map</p>
<table id="S6.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex65.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\dasharrow|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>given by</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\dasharrow|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}\quad\mbox{given by}\quad x\mapsto\{H:H\ni x\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm49.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\dasharrow|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{\mathcal{L}}:X\dasharrow|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the fixed part of
the linear system <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math>. However, as long as
<math id="Thmthm49.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="Thmthm49.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mobile,</span> that is,
the base locus of <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|_{k^{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|_{k^{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>
has codimension <math id="Thmthm49.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the isomorphism
(<a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>.2) holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm49.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm49.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, let <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:X\dasharrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:X\dasharrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a map between proper,
geometrically connected and geometrically reduced
varieties and <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> a
twisted line bundle on <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that either <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a morphism or
<math id="Thmthm49.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth. Then <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is a twisted line bundle on <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the induced pull-back map <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}:|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|\dasharrow|\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}:|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|\dasharrow|\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|</annotation></semantics></math> is twisted linear. Thus, by
(<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>.4),</p>
<table id="S6.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex66.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm49.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Working with rank 1 reflexive sheaves as in
Definition <a href="#Thmthm6" title="Variant Definitions 6. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that
(<a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>.4) holds provided <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> are normal and <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> makes sense, that is,
<math id="Thmthm49.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in the base locus of <math id="Thmthm49.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next we come to the definition of products. We start with the
standard version and then translate it into other forms.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm50.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 50</span></span><span id="Thmthm50.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Products)<span id="Thmthm50.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm50.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here are 5 versions of the definition of products.
The first 2 variants recall known definitions from geometry and algebra.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(Polarized pair version <a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.1) Let <math id="Thmthm50.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y</annotation></semantics></math> be proper
<math id="Thmthm50.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-varieties and <math id="Thmthm50.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{X},{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{X},{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> twisted line bundles on them.
Their product is</p>
<table id="S6.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(X\times Y,{\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y}:=\pi_{X}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{X}\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(X\times Y,{\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y}:=\pi_{X}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{X}\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm50.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmthm50.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{X},\pi_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{X},\pi_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinate projections.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(Algebra version <a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.2) It is clear that
<math id="Thmthm50.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{X}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{X}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\in T({\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is not necessarily
of minimal rank in <math id="Thmthm50.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T({\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T({\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y})</annotation></semantics></math>, but we have that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{End}\bigl(\pi_{X}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\bigr)\cong\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\bigr)\otimes\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{End}\bigl(\pi_{X}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\bigr)\cong\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}}_{X})\bigr)\otimes\operatorname{End}\bigl(E({\mathcal{L}}_{Y})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We can now turn (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.1) into a definition of products of linear systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(Linear system version <a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.3) Following the previous examples, we set</p>
<table id="S6.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}_{X}|\otimes|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|:=|{\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y}|=\bigl|\pi_{X}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{X}\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}_{X}|\otimes|{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}|:=|{\mathcal{L}}_{X\times Y}|=\bigl|\pi_{X}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{X}\otimes\pi_{Y}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{Y}\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Thinking of a Severi–Brauer variety as a twisted linear system
gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p7.1" class="ltx_p">(Severi–Brauer version <a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.4) A Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm50.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>
is isomorphic to <math id="Thmthm50.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(1)|</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests that the product
of Severi–Brauer varieties <math id="Thmthm50.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> should be defined as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\otimes Q:=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}\times Q^{\vee}}(1,1)|,\quad\mbox{which is isomorphic to}\quad|{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times Q}(1,1)|^{\vee}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>which is isomorphic to</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\otimes Q:=|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}\times Q^{\vee}}(1,1)|,\quad\mbox{which is isomorphic to}\quad|{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times Q}(1,1)|^{\vee},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm50.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Thmthm50.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times Q}(a,b):=\pi_{P}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(a)\otimes\pi_{Q}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>Q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times Q}(a,b):=\pi_{P}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(a)\otimes\pi_{Q}^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>
using the coordinate projections of <math id="Thmthm50.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will write
<math id="Thmthm50.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{\otimes m}:=P\otimes\cdots\otimes P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\otimes m}:=P\otimes\cdots\otimes P</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="Thmthm50.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> factors).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p8.1" class="ltx_p">One can reformulate (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.4) as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p9.1" class="ltx_p">(Segre embedding version <a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.5) Let <math id="Thmthm50.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm50.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be
Severi–Brauer varieties. Then <math id="Thmthm50.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q\cong P_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes P_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≅</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\cong P_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes P_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> iff there is an embedding</p>
<table id="S6.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j:P_{1}\times\cdots\times P_{r}\lhook\joinrel\to Q" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⸦→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:P_{1}\times\cdots\times P_{r}\lhook\joinrel\to Q</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm50.p9.2" class="ltx_p">that becomes the Segre embedding over <math id="Thmthm50.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm50.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm50.p10.1" class="ltx_p">To see that (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.4) and (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.5) are equivalent,
note that <math id="Thmthm50.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> gives a twisted linear pull-back map
<math id="Thmthm50.p10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j^{*}:Q^{\vee}\to|j^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\cong P_{1}^{\vee}\times\cdots\times P_{r}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≅</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}:Q^{\vee}\to|j^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\cong P_{1}^{\vee}\times\cdots\times P_{r}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is an isomorphism over <math id="Thmthm50.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{k}</annotation></semantics></math> hence also an isomorphism over <math id="Thmthm50.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm51" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm51.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 51</span></span><span id="Thmthm51.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm51.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm51.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm51.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper, geometrically reduced and connected <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety,
and <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> base-point free, twisted line bundles on <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|\sim\otimes_{i}|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|\sim\otimes_{i}|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>,
that is, the linear system associated to the tensor product
<math id="Thmthm51.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
is similar to the product of the linear systems <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="Thmthm51.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}^{m}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}^{m}|\sim|{\mathcal{L}}|^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we do not claim isomorphism, only similarity,
as in (<a href="#Thmthm42" title="Definition–Lemma 42. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Consider the diagonal embedding <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta:X\lhook\joinrel\to X^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⸦→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta:X\lhook\joinrel\to X^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the twisted line bundle <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}:=\otimes_{i}\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}:=\otimes_{i}\pi_{i}^{*}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinate projections.
Then <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta^{*}{\mathcal{M}}=\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}{\mathcal{M}}=\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and, using first
(<a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>.4) and then (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.4), we get that
<math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|=|\delta^{*}{\mathcal{M}}|\sim|{\mathcal{M}}|\sim\otimes_{i}|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>δ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\otimes_{i}{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|=|\delta^{*}{\mathcal{M}}|\sim|{\mathcal{M}}|\sim\otimes_{i}|{\mathcal{L}}_{i}|</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm52" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm52.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 52</span></span><span id="Thmthm52.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm52.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm52.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm52.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm52.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\dasharrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\dasharrow Q</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational map between
Severi–Brauer varieties. Then <math id="Thmthm52.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmthm52.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="Thmthm52.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\sim P^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\sim P^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Proof.
By (<a href="#Thmthm49" title="Definition 49. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>.4) we get
a linear embedding <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>⸦→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\lhook\joinrel\to|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter is
similar to <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm51" title="Lemma 51. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>). Thus
<math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> and taking duals gives that
<math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\sim P^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\sim P^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>.∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm53" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm53.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 53</span></span><span id="Thmthm53.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cremona transformations I)<span id="Thmthm53.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm53.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm53.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simplest birational transformation of <math id="Thmthm53.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S6.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S6.Ex72.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm53" title="Example 53 (Cremona transformations I). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x_{0}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n})\mapsto(x_{0}^{-1}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n}^{-1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n})\mapsto(x_{0}^{-1}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n}^{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm53.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After multiplying by <math id="Thmthm53.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{0}\cdots x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\cdots x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is given by the linear system of degree <math id="Thmthm53.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> polynomials that vanish with multiplicity <math id="Thmthm53.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> at the coordinate vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm53.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm53.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus if <math id="Thmthm53.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm53.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="Thmthm53.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> a 0-dimensional, spanning subscheme of degree <math id="Thmthm53.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmthm53.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n)(-(n-1)Z)\bigr|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(n)(-(n-1)Z)\bigr|</annotation></semantics></math> defines a birational map
<math id="Thmthm53.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm54" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm54.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 54</span></span><span id="Thmthm54.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cremona transformations II)<span id="Thmthm54.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm54.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm54.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A more precise version is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm54.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm54.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> be an extension of degree <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{K}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{K}\cong{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}\subset P_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}\subset P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}\subset P_{K}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}\subset P_{K}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> be hyperplanes whose conjugates <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) are linearly independent. Set <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=\sum H_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=\sum H_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm54.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:=\sum G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:=\sum G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm54.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm54.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi,\pi^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi,\pi^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the coordinate projections of <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#Thmthm47" title="Definition 47. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle,
and the <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> conjugates of <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}H_{0}+(\pi^{\vee})^{*}G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}H_{0}+(\pi^{\vee})^{*}G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> span a linear system that is defined over <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The intersection of all these conjugates <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{H,G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{H,G}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the graph of a Cremona transformation <math id="Thmthm54.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H,G}:P\dasharrow P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H,G}:P\dasharrow P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm54.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H,G}^{*}(G_{0})=H-H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H,G}^{*}(G_{0})=H-H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm54.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm54.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, this claim can be checked over <math id="Thmthm54.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then choose
<math id="Thmthm54.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}=(x_{i}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}=(x_{i}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm54.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}=(y_{i}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}=(y_{i}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="Thmthm54.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{H,G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{H,G}</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by the equations
<math id="Thmthm54.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}y_{0}=\cdots=x_{n}y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}y_{0}=\cdots=x_{n}y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the closed graph of (<a href="#Thmthm53" title="Example 53 (Cremona transformations I). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>.1).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm54.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm54.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let now <math id="Thmthm54.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}_{0}\subset P_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}_{0}\subset P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be another hyperplane. Then</p>
<table id="S6.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi_{H,G}\circ\phi_{G,H^{\prime}})^{*}(H^{\prime}_{0})=\phi_{H,G}^{*}(G-G_{0})=(n-1)H+H_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi_{H,G}\circ\phi_{G,H^{\prime}})^{*}(H^{\prime}_{0})=\phi_{H,G}^{*}(G-G_{0})=(n-1)H+H_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm54.p5.2" class="ltx_p">After canceling the common <math id="Thmthm54.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)H</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that
<math id="Thmthm54.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H,G}\circ\phi_{G,H^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H,G}\circ\phi_{G,H^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an automorphism of <math id="Thmthm54.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that maps
<math id="Thmthm54.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmthm54.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The definition of <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{L}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{L}}|</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to coherent sheaves.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm55" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm55.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 55</span></span><span id="Thmthm55.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm55.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm55.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a coherent sheaf on a Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="Thmthm55.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> a twisted line bundle. Then one can define a
Severi–Brauer variety</p>
<table id="S6.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F\otimes\mathcal{L}|\quad\mbox{such that}\quad|F\otimes\mathcal{L}|_{k^{s}}={\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F_{k^{s}}\otimes\mathcal{L})^{\vee}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>such that</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F\otimes\mathcal{L}|\quad\mbox{such that}\quad|F\otimes\mathcal{L}|_{k^{s}}={\mathbb{P}}\bigl(H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F_{k^{s}}\otimes\mathcal{L})^{\vee}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm55.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To do this, let <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a very ample line bundle on <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm55.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a quotient <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{i}A^{-1}\twoheadrightarrow F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{i}A^{-1}\twoheadrightarrow F</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S6.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L})\to H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L})\to H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm55.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is surjective.
Since <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is a direct sum of twisted line bundles, we get a Severi–Brauer structure on the
projectivization of <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},\oplus_{i}A^{r-1}\otimes\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn puts a
Severi–Brauer structure on
the projectivization of <math id="Thmthm55.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm55.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm55.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Choosing a general <math id="Thmthm55.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\lhook\joinrel\to A^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>⸦→</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\lhook\joinrel\to A^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> then realizes
<math id="Thmthm55.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math> as a
linear subspace of <math id="Thmthm55.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(P_{k^{s}},F\otimes A^{r}\otimes\mathcal{L})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>The Brauer group</h2>

<div id="Thmthm56" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm56.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 56</span></span><span id="Thmthm56.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm56.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm56.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm56.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The product defined in (<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.4) makes the
Brauer equivalence classes of Severi–Brauer varieties into a commutative group. The inverse of <math id="Thmthm56.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm56.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. The product defines a commutative monoid structure on
the isomorphism classes of Severi–Brauer varieties.
If <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\dasharrow Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\dasharrow Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are twisted linear maps, then so is
<math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}\otimes Q_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}\otimes Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}\otimes Q_{1}\dasharrow P_{2}\otimes Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we get a
commutative monoid on the Brauer equivalence classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As we noted in (<a href="#Thmthm47" title="Definition 47. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>),
<math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a (non–twisted) line bundle, hence
<math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{P\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)|</annotation></semantics></math> is a trivial Severi–Brauer variety.
This says that
<math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\otimes P^{\vee}\sim{\mathbb{P}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\otimes P^{\vee}\sim{\mathbb{P}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm57" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm57.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 57</span></span><span id="Thmthm57.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm57.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm57.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The group defined above is called the <span id="Thmthm57.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Brauer group</span> of the field <math id="Thmthm57.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and denoted by <math id="Thmthm57.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. It was originally defined in terms of
tensor products of (Brauer equivalence classes of)
central simple <math id="Thmthm57.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-algebras; see (<a href="#Thmthm11" title="Aside 11. ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Nowadays it is most commonly defined as
<math id="Thmthm57.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(k,{\mathbb{G}}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(k,{\mathbb{G}}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, the second Galois cohomology group of <math id="Thmthm57.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{G}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{G}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GSz06</a>]</cite> for these aspects.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm57.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm57.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The order of <math id="Thmthm57.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="Thmthm57.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is traditionally called that <span id="Thmthm57.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">period</span> of <math id="Thmthm57.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="Thmthm57.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\sim P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\sim P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>, they have the same period.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm58" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm58.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 58</span></span><span id="Thmthm58.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm58.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm58.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm58.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The period of <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> equals the smallest <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a (non-twisted) line bundle on <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the period of <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm58.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a torsion group.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. By (<a href="#Thmthm51" title="Lemma 51. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>), <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}\sim(P^{\vee})^{\otimes m}\sim(P^{\otimes m})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)|\sim|{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)|^{\otimes m}\sim(P^{\vee})^{\otimes m}\sim(P^{\otimes m})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As we noted in Example <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(\dim P+1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(-K_{P})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a (non-twisted) line bundle on <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm59" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm59.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 59</span></span><span id="Thmthm59.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Amitsur’s theorem)<span id="Thmthm59.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm59.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm59.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm59.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm59.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> be Severi–Brauer varieties.
The following are equivalent.</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(59.1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is similar to
</span><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some </span><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(59.2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(59.3)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a rational map </span><math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\dasharrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\dasharrow Q</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Assume (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.1) and let <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> be the function field of <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point
(the generic point of <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>) thus <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial and so is
<math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{K}\sim P_{K}^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{K}\sim P_{K}^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm14" title="Corollary 14 (Châtelet’s theorem). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), hence
(<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.2) holds. The implication (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.2) <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.3) is clear.
Finally assume that there is a rational map <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\dasharrow Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\dasharrow Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{Q}(1)\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> hence
<math id="S7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\sim P^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\sim P^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm52" title="Corollary 52. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Frequently (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.1) <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Leftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Leftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.2) is stated in the following equivalent form.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm60" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm60.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 60</span></span><span id="Thmthm60.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm60.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm60.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm60.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The kernel of the base-change map
<math id="Thmthm60.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}\bigl(k(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}\bigl(k(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is the subgroup generated by <math id="Thmthm60.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm61" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm61.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 61</span></span><span id="Thmthm61.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm61.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm61.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.1) <math id="Thmthm61.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm59" title="Proposition 59 (Amitsur’s theorem). ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">59</span></a>.2)
twice we obtain that if
<math id="Thmthm61.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> generate the same subgroup of <math id="Thmthm61.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> then</p>
<table id="S7.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times Q\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}\times Q." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim Q}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times Q\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}\times Q.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm61.p1.2" class="ltx_p">That is, <math id="Thmthm61.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm61.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are stably birational to each other.
As a special case,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P\times{\mathbb{P}}^{\dim P}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm61.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm61.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is an unsolved problem whether the stronger assertion
<math id="Thmthm61.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm61.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm61.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Severi–Brauer varieties that become trivial after a cyclic Galois extension, the birational isomorphism
<math id="Thmthm61.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from (<a href="#Thmthm79" title="79Complement to (). ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>.2).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm61.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm61.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Besides Cremona transformations as in (<a href="#Thmthm53" title="Example 53 (Cremona transformations I). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>)
the only other known general birational isomorphisms among Severi–Brauer varieties is the following.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm62" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_exmp">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm62.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 62</span></span><span id="Thmthm62.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm62.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm62.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tre91</a>]</cite>
Let <math id="Thmthm62.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be an odd dimensional <math id="Thmthm62.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vectorspace and <math id="Thmthm62.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{char}k\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>char</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{char}k\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can think of <math id="Thmthm62.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{2}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{2}V</annotation></semantics></math> is skew-symmetric matrices and a general
skew-symmetric matrix has a unique eigenvector with eigenvalue 0.
This gives a rational map <math id="Thmthm62.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{2}V\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}(V)^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{2}V\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}(V)^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm62.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm62.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For an even dimensional
Severi–Brauer variety <math id="Thmthm62.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, define
<math id="Thmthm62.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\wedge^{2}P\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∧</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊂</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge^{2}P\subset|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}\times P^{\vee}}(1,1)|</annotation></semantics></math> as the sections that vanish on the diagonal. Thus there is a rational map
<math id="Thmthm62.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi:\wedge^{2}P\dasharrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\wedge^{2}P\dasharrow P</annotation></semantics></math>.
This map is not twisted linear, but its fibers are linear
subspaces. Thus if <math id="Thmthm62.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Q\subset\wedge^{2}P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊂</mo><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\subset\wedge^{2}P</annotation></semantics></math> is a minimal twisted linear
subspace, then <math id="Thmthm62.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi|_{Q}:Q\dasharrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi|_{Q}:Q\dasharrow P</annotation></semantics></math> is birational.
Therefore
<math id="Thmthm62.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P^{\otimes 2})^{\rm min}\cong Q\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>min</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><mi>Q</mi><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P^{\otimes 2})^{\rm min}\cong Q\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm62.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm62.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are many numbers <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> generate the
multiplicative group <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{Z}}/n)^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{Z}}/n)^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we obtain that if
<math id="Thmthm62.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is such a number, and
<math id="Thmthm62.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> generate the same subgroup of order <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmthm62.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="Thmthm62.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Index of a Severi–Brauer variety</h2>

<div id="Thmthm63" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm63.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 63</span></span><span id="Thmthm63.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Index of a variety)<span id="Thmthm63.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm63.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm63.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm63.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper <math id="Thmthm63.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-scheme.
The <span id="Thmthm63.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">index</span> of <math id="Thmthm63.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the gcd of the degrees of all
0-cycles on <math id="Thmthm63.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is denoted by <math id="Thmthm63.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm63.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm63.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For smooth, proper varieties the index is a birational invariant by (<a href="#Thmthm64" title="Lemma 64 (Nishimura lemma). ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">64</span></a>).
More general indices are defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kol13</a>]</cite>, building on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">ELW15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A short proof of the following is in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">KSC04</a>, p.183]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm64" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm64.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 64</span></span><span id="Thmthm64.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nishimura lemma)<span id="Thmthm64.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm64.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm64.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm64.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\dasharrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\dasharrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational map
between <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-varieties. Assume that <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a smooth <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point and <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is proper. Then <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="Thmthm64.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-point. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm65" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm65.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 65</span></span><span id="Thmthm65.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm65.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm65.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm65.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm65.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a normal, proper <math id="Thmthm65.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-variety
and <math id="Thmthm65.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> a finite field extension. Then
<math id="Thmthm65.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(X_{k})\mid\deg(K/k)\cdot\operatorname{index}(X_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(X_{k})\mid\deg(K/k)\cdot\operatorname{index}(X_{K})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:X_{K}\to X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:X_{K}\to X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite map whose degree equals
<math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus if <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is a 0-cycle on <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(p_{*}Z)=\deg(K/k)\cdot\deg Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(p_{*}Z)=\deg(K/k)\cdot\deg Z</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm66" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm66.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 66</span></span><span id="Thmthm66.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm66.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm66.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm66.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm66.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> be Severi–Brauer varieties. Then
<math id="Thmthm66.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P\otimes Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P\otimes Q)</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="Thmthm66.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\cdot\operatorname{index}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\cdot\operatorname{index}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="Thmthm66.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="Thmthm66.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Let <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{P}\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{P}\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{Q}\subset Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{Q}\subset Q</annotation></semantics></math> be 0-cycles.
Then <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{P}\times Z_{Q}\subset P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{P}\times Z_{Q}\subset P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a 0-cycle
whose degree is <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(Z_{P})\cdot\deg(Z_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(Z_{P})\cdot\deg(Z_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. By
(<a href="#Thmthm50" title="Definition 50 (Products). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>.5) it is also a 0-cycle on <math id="S8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\otimes Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\otimes Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The diagonal embedding shows that <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a 0-cycle on
<math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{m}\subset P^{\otimes m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{m}\subset P^{\otimes m}</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm67" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm67.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 67</span></span><span id="Thmthm67.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm67.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm67.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm67.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmthm67.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> generate the same subgroup of <math id="Thmthm67.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> then they have the same index. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm68" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm68.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 68</span></span><span id="Thmthm68.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm68.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm68.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm68.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm68.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(68.1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.1389em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(68.2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)=\operatorname{index}(P^{\rm min})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)=\operatorname{index}(P^{\rm min})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(68.3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)=\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)=\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(68.4)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains smooth, 0-dimensional subschemes whose degree equals </span><math id="S8.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. By (<a href="#Thmthm36" title="Theorem 36. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>), <math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> contains a 0-cycle of degree <math id="S8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math>, so (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>.1) holds by definition.
By (<a href="#Thmthm39" title="Proposition 39. ‣ 5. Non-minimal Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>.4) <math id="S8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\rm min}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mover><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\stackrel{{\scriptstyle bir}}{{\sim}}P^{\rm min}\times{\mathbb{P}}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>
hence any 0-cycle on <math id="S8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>) yields a
0-cycle of the same degree on <math id="S8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>).
This shows (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>.2).</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#Thmthm36" title="Theorem 36. ‣ 4. Splitting fields ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>), <math id="S8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
contain smooth, <math id="S8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subschemes of degree <math id="S8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\leq\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\leq\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally let <math id="S8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be a 0-dimensional reduced subscheme.
By (<a href="#Thmthm55" title="Definition 55. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>), <math id="S8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{Z}(-1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{Z}(-1)|</annotation></semantics></math> is a Severi–Brauer variety similar to <math id="S8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its dimension is <math id="S8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg Z-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg Z-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg Z</annotation></semantics></math>
is divisible by
<math id="S8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>.3) and proves (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>.4). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm69" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm69.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 69</span></span><span id="Thmthm69.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm69.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm69.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm69.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm69.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety and <math id="Thmthm69.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> a divisor of <math id="Thmthm69.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="Thmthm69.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="Thmthm69.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{m}\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{m}\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Write <math id="S8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=m_{1}m_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=m_{1}m_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S8.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m}\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m}\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>
then either <math id="S8.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m_{2}}\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m_{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m_{2}}\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m_{1}})</annotation></semantics></math>
or <math id="S8.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m_{1}})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m_{1}}\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes m_{1}})\nmid\tfrac{1}{m_{1}}\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus it is enough to prove the claim when <math id="S8.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=p</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime. Write <math id="S8.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)=p^{a}c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)=p^{a}c</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S8.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,c)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,c)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>) we may assume that <math id="S8.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P=p^{a}c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P=p^{a}c-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#Thmthm55" title="Definition 55. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\otimes r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\otimes r}</annotation></semantics></math> is similar to <math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(p)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{O}}_{P^{\vee}}(p)|</annotation></semantics></math>,
which has dimension <math id="S8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tbinom{p^{a}c+p-1}{p}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tbinom{p^{a}c+p-1}{p}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S8.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{a-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{a-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest <math id="S8.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-power dividing <math id="S8.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tbinom{p^{a}c+p-1}{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tbinom{p^{a}c+p-1}{p}</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is also the largest <math id="S8.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-power dividing
<math id="S8.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P^{\otimes p})</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>). ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm70" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm70.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 70</span></span><span id="Thmthm70.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm70.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm70.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm70.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm70.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety. Then</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{period}(P)\mid\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{\dim P}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>period</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>period</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{period}(P)\mid\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{\dim P}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm70.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm70.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the period and the index have the same prime factors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. By (<a href="#Thmthm58" title="Proposition 58. ‣ 7. The Brauer group ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">58</span></a>) the period of <math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same period,
the period of <math id="S8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P^{\rm min}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P^{\rm min}+1</annotation></semantics></math> and the latter equals
<math id="S8.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle then intersecting the zero set of <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>
general sections yields a 0-cycle of degree <math id="S8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\dim P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\dim P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <math id="S8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{\dim P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>period</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{\dim P}</annotation></semantics></math>.∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Combining (<a href="#Thmthm70" title="Corollary 70. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>) with (<a href="#Thmthm65" title="Lemma 65. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">65</span></a>) gives the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm71" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm71.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 71</span></span><span id="Thmthm71.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm71.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm71.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm71.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm71.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite field extension. Then
the kernel of the base-change map <math id="Thmthm71.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Br</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="Thmthm71.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aside <a href="#Thmthm71" title="Lemma 71. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>.1.</span> The <span id="S8.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">period-index problem</span> asks to determine the smallest
number <math id="S8.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on the field <math id="S8.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) such that
<math id="S8.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>period</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\operatorname{period}(P)^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>
for every Severi–Brauer variety <math id="S8.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S8.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">dJ04</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SdJ10</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lie15</a>]</cite> for various results.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm72" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm72.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 72</span></span><span id="Thmthm72.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Primary decomposition)<span id="Thmthm72.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm72.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm72.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm72.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm72.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimal
Severi–Brauer variety. Write <math id="Thmthm72.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P+1=\prod_{i}p_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P+1=\prod_{i}p_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> where the <math id="Thmthm72.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
are distinct primes. Then there are unique, minimal Severi–Brauer varieties
<math id="Thmthm72.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm72.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P_{i}+1=p_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P_{i}+1=p_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmthm72.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Set <math id="S8.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:=\dim P+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:=\dim P+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=p_{i}^{-c_{i}}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=p_{i}^{-c_{i}}a</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S8.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\textstyle{\sum}_{i}e_{i}a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\textstyle{\sum}_{i}e_{i}a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and note that <math id="S8.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\equiv e_{i}a_{i}\mod p_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>mod</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\equiv e_{i}a_{i}\mod p_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Assume first that <math id="S8.p15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#Thmthm70" title="Corollary 70. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>)
the period of <math id="S8.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}^{c_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}^{c_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S8.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim{\mathbb{P}}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim{\mathbb{P}}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S8.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</p>
<table id="S8.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim\otimes_{j}P_{j}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim P_{i}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim P_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim\otimes_{j}P_{j}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim P_{i}^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\sim P_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p15.2" class="ltx_p">hence the <math id="S8.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are unique. Conversely, set
<math id="S8.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}=\bigl(P^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\bigr)^{\rm min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>min</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}=\bigl(P^{\otimes e_{i}a_{i}}\bigr)^{\rm min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S8.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\sim P^{\otimes\sum e_{i}a_{i}}\sim\otimes_{i}P_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">e</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\sim P^{\otimes\sum e_{i}a_{i}}\sim\otimes_{i}P_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p15.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S8.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal, this shows that
<math id="S8.p15.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0em">≅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cong\otimes_{i}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> iff <math id="S8.p15.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dim P\geq\dim\otimes_{i}P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P\geq\dim\otimes_{i}P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The period of <math id="S8.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm70" title="Corollary 70. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>),
hence the period of <math id="S8.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the index of <math id="S8.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is
also a <math id="S8.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-power by (<a href="#Thmthm70" title="Corollary 70. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>).
On the other hand, by (<a href="#Thmthm66" title="Lemma 66. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">66</span></a>) the index of <math id="S8.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
divides <math id="S8.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the index of <math id="S8.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S8.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore
<math id="S8.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim P_{i}\leq p_{i}^{c_{i}}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim P_{i}\leq p_{i}^{c_{i}}-1</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm68" title="Theorem 68. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">68</span></a>.3) hence
<math id="S8.p16.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dim\otimes_{i}P_{i}\leq\dim P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\otimes_{i}P_{i}\leq\dim P</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm73" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm73.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 73</span></span><span id="Thmthm73.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm73.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm73.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm73.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset P</annotation></semantics></math> be a 1-dimensional subscheme. Applying
(<a href="#Thmthm55" title="Definition 55. ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) to <math id="Thmthm73.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F={\mathcal{O}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F={\mathcal{O}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm73.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> to get that
<math id="Thmthm73.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\pm\deg C+\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\pm\deg C+\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C}).</annotation></semantics></math>
This shows that</p>
<ol id="S8.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(73.1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\deg C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\deg C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S8.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(73.2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\deg C+\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\deg C+\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\operatorname{index}(P)\mid\chi({\mathcal{O}}_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> if
<math id="S8.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{index}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>index</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{index}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm73.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These results are optimal, but I do not know how to get
necessary and sufficient conditions on the Hilbert polynomials of higher dimensional subvarieties.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct
Severi–Brauer varieties from degree <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> hypersurfaces in <math id="S9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that are defined using norm forms (<a href="#Thmthm19" title="Definition 19 (Norm forms). ‣ 2. First examples of Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) of cyclic Galois extensions.
The main result is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm74" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm74.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 74</span></span><span id="Thmthm74.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm74.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm74.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm74.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a cyclic Galois extension <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that has a <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point.
Then <math id="Thmthm74.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is birational to a hypersurface of the form</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S9.Ex81.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(K/k,e):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})=et^{n}\bigr)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}\quad\mbox{for some}\quad e\in k^{\times}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for some</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(K/k,e):=\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})=et^{n}\bigr)\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}\quad\mbox{for some}\quad e\in k^{\times},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm74.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm74.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and every such hypersurface is birational to at least one Severi–Brauer variety.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A more precise version is outlined in (<a href="#Thmthm79" title="79Complement to (). ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm75" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_aside">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm75.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aside 75</span></span><span id="Thmthm75.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm75.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm75.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The elements of norm 1 form a <math id="Thmthm75.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-subtorus
<math id="Thmthm75.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{n-1}\subset K^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n-1}\subset K^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>. This torus acts on <math id="Thmthm75.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmthm75.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{0}(K/k,e):=X(K/k,e)\setminus(t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{0}(K/k,e):=X(K/k,e)\setminus(t=0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a
torsor under <math id="Thmthm75.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will see that the birational maps
<math id="Thmthm75.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(K/k,e)\dasharrow P:=P(K/k,e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(K/k,e)\dasharrow P:=P(K/k,e,r)</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphisms over <math id="Thmthm75.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{0}(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{0}(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and
the <math id="Thmthm75.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K/k,e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K/k,e,r)</annotation></semantics></math> inherit this
<math id="Thmthm75.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm76" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm76.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">76</span></span><span id="Thmthm76.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First part of the proof.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm76.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm76.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that has a <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point. Pick a <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P(K)</annotation></semantics></math> such that its conjugates
<math id="Thmthm76.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=p,\dots,p_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=p,\dots,p_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> span <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#Thmthm54" title="Example 54 (Cremona transformations II). ‣ 6. Twisted linear systems ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) two such points <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> differ by an automorphism of <math id="Thmthm76.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, so this is not an additional choice.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm76.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm76.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix also a generator <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives a cyclic order on the points <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; we may assume that it is <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(p_{i})=p_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(p_{i})=p_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</p>
<table id="S9.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}:=\langle p_{1},\dots,p_{i-1},\widehat{p_{i}},p_{i+1},\dots,p_{n}\rangle\subset P_{K}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}:=\langle p_{1},\dots,p_{i-1},\widehat{p_{i}},p_{i+1},\dots,p_{n}\rangle\subset P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p2.2" class="ltx_p">denote the hyperplane that contains the points <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{j}:j\neq i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{j}:j\neq i\}</annotation></semantics></math> and choose an equation <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{i}=(m_{i}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{i}=(m_{i}=0)</annotation></semantics></math> (also over <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>).
Consider the vectorspace of functions spanned by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\tfrac{m_{1}}{m_{n}},\tfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}},\cdots,\tfrac{m_{n}}{m_{n-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\tfrac{m_{1}}{m_{n}},\tfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}},\cdots,\tfrac{m_{n}}{m_{n-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Note that although <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(H_{i})=H_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(H_{i})=H_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is usually not possible to choose the <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}(m_{i+1})=m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}(m_{i+1})=m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. However, no matter how the <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are chosen, <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{*}(m_{i+1})=\lambda_{i}m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{*}(m_{i+1})=\lambda_{i}m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\in K^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\in K^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the induced linear system on <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
This gives a rational map</p>
<table id="S9.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S9.Ex84.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm76" title="76First part of the proof. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">76</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:P\dasharrow X\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:P\dasharrow X\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This may be clearer if we think of <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> as given by the linear system spanned by the hypersurfaces</p>
<table id="S9.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{array}[]{l}H_{1}+\cdots+H_{n}\quad\mbox{and}\\
H_{1}+\cdots+H_{i-2}+2H_{i}+H_{i+1}+\cdots+H_{n}\quad\mbox{for}\quad i=1,\dots,n.\end{array}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{array}[]{l}H_{1}+\cdots+H_{n}\quad\mbox{and}\\
H_{1}+\cdots+H_{i-2}+2H_{i}+H_{i+1}+\cdots+H_{n}\quad\mbox{for}\quad i=1,\dots,n.\end{array}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p2.5" class="ltx_p">The first of them is defined over <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the others are permuted by <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>. So the linear system, and hence <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, are defined over <math id="Thmthm76.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm76.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm76.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We need to show that <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is birational and <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has the required equation.
Birationality of a map can be checked after a field extension.
Over <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> we can choose <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}:=m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}:=m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as coordinates, and then the map <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{K}:{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}_{\mathbf{u}}\cong P_{K}\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msubsup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{K}:{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}_{\mathbf{u}}\cong P_{K}\dasharrow{\mathbb{P}}^{n}_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(u_{1}{:}\dots{:}u_{n})\mapsto\bigl(1{:}\tfrac{u_{1}}{u_{n}}{:}\tfrac{u_{2}}{u_{1}}{:}\cdots{:}\tfrac{u_{n}}{u_{n-1}}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1}{:}\dots{:}u_{n})\mapsto\bigl(1{:}\tfrac{u_{1}}{u_{n}}{:}\tfrac{u_{2}}{u_{1}}{:}\cdots{:}\tfrac{u_{n}}{u_{n-1}}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p3.2" class="ltx_p">whose image satisfies the obvious equation
<math id="Thmthm76.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=x_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=x_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Over <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the inverse is given by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(1{:}x_{1}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n})\mapsto\bigl(x_{1}{:}(x_{1}x_{2}){:}\cdots{:}(x_{1}\cdots x_{n})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1{:}x_{1}{:}\cdots{:}x_{n})\mapsto\bigl(x_{1}{:}(x_{1}x_{2}){:}\cdots{:}(x_{1}\cdots x_{n})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm76.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, starting with a Severi–Brauer variety of dimension <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that has a <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-point, plus a generator of <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a
hypersurface <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-form of <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=x_{0}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=x_{0}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, plus a cyclic
order on its points of multiplicity <math id="Thmthm76.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm76.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm76.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next we check that the equation of <math id="Thmthm76.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written in the form (<a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>.1).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm77" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm77.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 77</span></span><span id="Thmthm77.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm77.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm77.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm77.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a field and
<math id="Thmthm77.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> a hypersurface. Assume that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{k^{s}}\cong(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=t^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k^{s}}\cong(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=t^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm77.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm77.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then there is a degree <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> separable <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in k^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in k^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cong\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=et^{n}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cong\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=et^{n}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm77.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm77.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, <math id="Thmthm77.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined up to isomorphism, and
<math id="Thmthm77.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined as an element of
<math id="Thmthm77.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Note that <math id="S9.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=t^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}\cdots x_{n}=t^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points of multiplicity <math id="S9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and they span a hyperplane. Thus the same holds for <math id="S9.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can choose <math id="S9.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> the be the equation of this hyperplane.
Then <math id="S9.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap(t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap(t=0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of <math id="S9.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-planes in <math id="S9.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}\cong(t=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}\cong(t=0)</annotation></semantics></math>, thus its equation is of the form <math id="S9.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=0\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})=0\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S9.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S9.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points of multiplicity <math id="S9.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the equation</p>
<table id="S9.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})+L({\mathbf{x}})t^{n-1}=et^{n}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}({\mathbf{x}})+L({\mathbf{x}})t^{n-1}=et^{n}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S9.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is linear. By assumption <math id="S9.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes over <math id="S9.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S9.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next we write down the construction of <math id="S9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> starting from <math id="S9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm78" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm78.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">78</span></span><span id="Thmthm78.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second part of the proof.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm78.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm78.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="Thmthm78.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with coordinates <math id="Thmthm78.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(u_{1}{:}\cdots{:}u_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em">:</mo><mo lspace="0.111em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1}{:}\cdots{:}u_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to write down its anticanonical embedding using the <math id="Thmthm78.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=u_{i}/u_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=u_{i}/u_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm78.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm78.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding is given by monomials <math id="Thmthm78.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmthm78.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm78.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum c_{i}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum c_{i}=n</annotation></semantics></math>. We can scale these by <math id="Thmthm78.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{1}\cdots u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}\cdots u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
thus work with the monomials <math id="Thmthm78.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm78.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm78.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The recipe is the following. Find a string of the form</p>
<table id="S9.Ex91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(c_{i},c_{i+1},\dots,c_{j-1},c_{j})=(0,1,\dots,1,&gt;1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{i},c_{i+1},\dots,c_{j-1},c_{j})=(0,1,\dots,1,&gt;1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm78.p3.2" class="ltx_p">divide <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{j}/u_{i}=x_{j}x_{j-1}\cdots x_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}/u_{i}=x_{j}x_{j-1}\cdots x_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and repeat. At the end we write <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∏</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod u_{i}^{c_{i}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as a monomial in the <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The recipe is invariant under cyclic permutations of the <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}=\prod_{i}\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}=\prod_{i}\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then replacing the <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the
<math id="Thmthm78.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a linear system that is Galois invariant. Thus it defines the required inverse of <math id="Thmthm78.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmthm76" title="76First part of the proof. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">76</span></a>.1). ∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm79" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm79.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">79</span></span><span id="Thmthm79.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complement to (<a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>).</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm79.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm79.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In (<a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>)
the set of such Severi–Brauer varieties forms the group
<math id="Thmthm79.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ker[\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker[\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="Thmthm79.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-torsion by (<a href="#Thmthm71" title="Lemma 71. ‣ 8. Index of a Severi–Brauer variety ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>).
The isomorphism classes of the hypersurfaces is determined by
<math id="Thmthm79.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})</annotation></semantics></math> by (<a href="#Thmthm77" title="Lemma 77. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">77</span></a>).
These two groups are isomorphic, but (<a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>) gives a slightly twisted version of this isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm79.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm79.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proofs of the following are left to the reader. Most are straightforward, but (<a href="#Thmthm79" title="79Complement to (). ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>.7) is cumbersome.</p>
<ol id="S9.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction gives an isomorphism</p>
<table id="S9.Ex92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ker[\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)]\times({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}\cong\bigl(k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})\bigr)\times({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Br</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker[\operatorname{Br}(k)\to\operatorname{Br}(K)]\times({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}\cong\bigl(k^{\times}/\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})\bigr)\times({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus
for every hypersurface as in (<a href="#Thmthm74" title="Theorem 74. ‣ 9. Norm forms and Severi–Brauer varieties ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>.1) we get birationally equivalent
Severi–Brauer varieties <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K/k,e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K/k,e,r)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S9.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K/k,e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K/k,e,r)</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial over <math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> iff
<math id="S9.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>norm</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>×</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\operatorname{norm}_{K/k}(K^{\times})</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.3)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K/k,e,r)\sim P(K/k,e,1)^{\otimes r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K/k,e,r)\sim P(K/k,e,1)^{\otimes r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmthm79.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The construction of the Severi–Brauer varieties from the
hypersurface using blow-ups and contractions is given by the following steps.</p>
<ol id="S9.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S9.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.4)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The divisor <math id="S9.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:=(t=0)\subset X(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:=(t=0)\subset X(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S9.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> geometric irreducible components. Choosing a generator of <math id="S9.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}(K/k)</annotation></semantics></math> fixes a cyclic order
<math id="S9.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1},\dots,D_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1},\dots,D_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.5)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S9.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> geometric points of multiplicity <math id="S9.I2.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S9.I2.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:Y(K/k,e)\to X(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:Y(K/k,e)\to X(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math> denote their blow-up
with exceptional divisor <math id="S9.I2.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.6)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> geometric irreducible components <math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> naturally indexed by
ordered pairs <math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(i,j):1\leq i\neq j\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(i,j):1\leq i\neq j\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible components
<math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.I2.i6.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{r}=\cup_{i}E(i,i-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}=\cup_{i}E(i,i-r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(79.7)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S9.I2.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>×</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in({\mathbb{Z}}/n{\mathbb{Z}})^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math>, the image of <math id="S9.I2.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=Y(K/k,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=Y(K/k,e)</annotation></semantics></math> by the linear system</p>
<table id="S9.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|-K_{Y}+\tfrac{(n+1)(n-2)}{2}\bigl(\pi^{*}H-E_{r}\bigr)\bigr|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|-K_{Y}+\tfrac{(n+1)(n-2)}{2}\bigl(\pi^{*}H-E_{r}\bigr)\bigr|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.I2.i7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is an anticanonically embedded Severi–Brauer variety <math id="S9.I2.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(K/k,e,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(K/k,e,r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>Severi–Brauer schemes</h2>

<div id="Thmthm80" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm80.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 80</span></span><span id="Thmthm80.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm80.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm80.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="Thmthm80.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Severi–Brauer scheme</span> is a
smooth, proper morphism <math id="Thmthm80.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\to S</annotation></semantics></math> all of whose fibers are
Severi–Brauer varieties. Note that <math id="Thmthm80.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{P/S}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{P/S}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmthm80.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-ample, hence
<math id="Thmthm80.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is projective.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to show that the basic set-up of Assertion <a href="#Thmthm1" title="Assertion 1. ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
generalizes to Severi–Brauer schemes. The vector bundle <math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, or rather its dual, appears in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qui73</a>, §8.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm81" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm81.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 81</span></span><span id="Thmthm81.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm81.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm81.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm81.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm81.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:P\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:P\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer scheme.</span></p>
<ol id="S10.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(81.1)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a unique non-split extension</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P/S}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to F(P)\to T_{P/S}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">that induces the extension given in (</span><a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="S10.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.1) on all fibers.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(81.2)</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a locally free sheaf of algebras
whose fiber over </span><math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the central simple algebra corresponding to </span><math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)^{\rm opp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>opp</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)^{\rm opp}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the
<span id="S10.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Azumaya algebra</span> corresponding to <math id="S10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>; taking the opposite is just a
convention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Proof.
We aim to extend the method of Example <a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to
Severi–Brauer schemes.
A priori, the extensions in (<a href="#Thmthm5" title="Main Example 5 (
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                O
                P
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  1
                  )
                
              
            
          
         of a Severi–Brauer variety). ‣ 1. Geometrically split vector bundles ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.1) tell us only about the fibers, so we should look for a non-split extension</p>
<table id="S10.Ex95" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex95.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm81" title="Theorem 81. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to p^{*}L\to F(P)\to T_{P/S}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to p^{*}L\to F(P)\to T_{P/S}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is some line bundle on <math id="S10.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
The extension class is</p>
<table id="S10.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex96.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm81" title="Theorem 81. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{P/S},p^{*}L)=H^{1}(P,p^{*}L\otimes\Omega^{1}_{P/S})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Ext</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\operatorname{Ext}^{1}(T_{P/S},p^{*}L)=H^{1}(P,p^{*}L\otimes\Omega^{1}_{P/S}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S10.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence</p>
<table id="S10.Ex97" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex97.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm81" title="Theorem 81. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(P,p^{*}L\otimes\Omega^{1}_{P/S})=H^{0}\bigl(S,L\otimes R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(P,p^{*}L\otimes\Omega^{1}_{P/S})=H^{0}\bigl(S,L\otimes R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S10.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(P_{s},\Omega^{1}_{P_{s}})=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(P_{s},\Omega^{1}_{P_{s}})=k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{i}(P_{s},\Omega^{1}_{P_{s}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{i}(P_{s},\Omega^{1}_{P_{s}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for every fiber,
<math id="S10.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle. Thus we need to take</p>
<table id="S10.Ex98" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex98.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm81" title="Theorem 81. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:=\bigl(R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\bigr)^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:=\bigl(R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\bigr)^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p3.5" class="ltx_p">We check in (<a href="#Thmthm83" title="Corollary 83. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">83</span></a>) that
<math id="S10.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}p_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence in fact we do have an extension as in
(<a href="#Thmthm81" title="Theorem 81. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a>.1) and the formation of <math id="S10.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(P)</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with arbitrary base change.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally note that <math id="S10.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally free sheaf on
<math id="S10.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> whose restriction to every geometric fiber is a sum of copies of <math id="S10.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{P_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the formation of <math id="S10.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝓃</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{*}{\mathchanc{End}}\bigl(F(P)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> commutes with arbitrary base change.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In order to understand <math id="S10.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need a
better way of writing the Euler sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm82" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_say">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm82.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">82</span></span><span id="Thmthm82.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euler sequence, functorially.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm82.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm82.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For later purposes we work with <math id="Thmthm82.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundles.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm82.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm82.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be a locally free sheaf of rank <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:P:={\mathbb{P}}_{S}(W)\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:P:={\mathbb{P}}_{S}(W)\to S</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use projectivization as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har77</a>, p.162]</cite>,
thus there are natural identifications</p>
<table id="S10.Ex99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex99.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)\quad\mbox{and}\quad P\cong\bigl(W^{*}\setminus\{0\}\bigr)/{\mathbb{G}}_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}(1)\quad\mbox{and}\quad P\cong\bigl(W^{*}\setminus\{0\}\bigr)/{\mathbb{G}}_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm82.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The cotangent version of the Euler sequence
is the sheafification of the graded module homomorphism</p>
<table id="S10.Ex100" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex100.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}:W\otimes\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W^{*})[-1]\to\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W^{*}),\quad\mbox{where}\quad e^{*}(w\otimes w^{*}):=w(w^{*})\in{\mathcal{O}}_{S}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}:W\otimes\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W^{*})[-1]\to\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W^{*}),\quad\mbox{where}\quad e^{*}(w\otimes w^{*}):=w(w^{*})\in{\mathcal{O}}_{S},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm82.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmthm82.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Sym</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sym}_{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the symmetric algebra of <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes shifting degrees by 1.
Thus the functorial cotangent Euler sequence is</p>
<table id="S10.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex101.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\Omega_{P/S}^{1}\to\pi^{*}W\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P/S}(-1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e^{*}}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\Omega_{P/S}^{1}\to\pi^{*}W\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P/S}(-1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e^{*}}}{{\to}}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm82.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The dual tangent version is</p>
<table id="S10.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex102.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\pi^{*}W^{*}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P/S}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{P/S}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>e</mi></mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}\pi^{*}W^{*}\otimes{\mathcal{O}}_{P/S}(1)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}T_{P/S}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm82.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Both are unchanged if we tensor <math id="Thmthm82.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with a line bundle.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm82.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm82.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Pushing the latter forward gives</p>
<table id="S10.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left"><math id="S10.Ex103.m2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" intent=":literal"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a></mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}W^{*}\otimes W\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\pi_{*}T_{P/S}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>e</mi></mover><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>∂</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to{\mathcal{O}}_{S}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\to}}W^{*}\otimes W\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\partial}}{{\to}}\pi_{*}T_{P/S}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm83" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm83.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 83</span></span><span id="Thmthm83.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm83.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm83.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm83.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmthm83.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:P\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:P\to S</annotation></semantics></math> be a Severi–Brauer scheme.
Then <math id="Thmthm83.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically isomorphic to <math id="Thmthm83.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Proof. Assume first that <math id="S10.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:P\to S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:P\to S</annotation></semantics></math> is the projectivization of a vector bundle <math id="S10.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S10.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Pushing forward (<a href="#Thmthm82" title="82Euler sequence, functorially. ‣ 10. Severi–Brauer schemes ‣ Severi–Brauer varieties;a geometric treatment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>.3) gives a canonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="S10.Ex104" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\cong\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{S}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{1}\pi_{*}\Omega^{1}_{P/S}\cong\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{P}\cong{\mathcal{O}}_{S},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which is unchanged if we tensor <math id="S10.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with a line bundle.
We are done since a sheaf that has a canonical trivialization étale locally is trivial. ∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_bibliography">References</h2>

<ul class="ltx_biblist">
      
<li id="bib.bibx1" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_bibitem">[AHVAV17]</span>
